\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers on rosy US earnings reports.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 13:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 14 (Reuters) - The dollar forged higher across the board on Tuesday, hitting one-week highs against the euro and the yen, as U.S. earnings optimism prompted a rebound in the greenback after seven straight weeks of decline.  \par \par \uc2 After weeks of concentrating on the U.S. current account deficit - and the need for a weaker dollar to correct it - investors switched focus to the brighter outlook for Corporate America as Bank of America and Merrill Lynch posted pleasing results.  \par \par \uc2 Major U.S. bourses were poised for a firm open after rising on Monday to their highest level in over a year.  \par \par \uc2 "U.S. earnings releases are looking good and the focus has switched back to long-term investment returns," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS Treasury Services.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback leapt more than one percent to 110.25 yen, on track for its biggest one-day rise since late June, as speculative traders scrambled to exit long yen/short dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also up more than half a percent against the euro at $1.1635, but short of peaks set earlier in the session.  \par \par \uc2 News German economic sentiment fell unexpectedly in October had little impact on the euro, with many analysts noting that weak data tends to support bond markets, which in turn support the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ZEW index fell 0.6 points to 60.3, marking its first drop in 10 months.  \par \par \uc2 DUISENBERG STARTED IT  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's recovery started on Monday after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said a Group of Seven call last month for exchange rate flexibility was not directed at the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 statement was widely perceived as a demand that Asian countries, which are largely dependent on exports, let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, but the subsequent weakness in the dollar also boosted the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the euro rose to within one cent of its record high of $1.1932 hit earlier in the year. Many traders think euro long positions had accumulated to the extent that an adjustment was inevitable.  \par \par \uc2 "People are as bullish on the dollar today as they were bearish last week," said a London-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 But some traders took Duisenberg's comments as a sign that European policymakers were unhappy with the euro's rise and were ready to rein it in.  \par \par \uc2 EU Economic and Monetary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes was quoted in an Italian newspaper on Tuesday saying the euro's rise could help confirm European recovery next year and could allow easing of monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 FOCUS ON BUSH VISIT  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was also helped by talk that Japan was still selling yen to curb its recent export-hurting appreciation. Japan has already thrown more than 13.5 trillion yen at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year in a bid to slow the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Traders noted there was continued uncertainty about Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ahead of U.S. President George W. Bush's visit to Japan on Friday. Bush is expected to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he meets Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi.  \par \par \uc2 If Bush preaches the virtues of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, that would be taken as indirect criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would lift the yen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Bush may say he believes in a strong dollar, giving a shot in the arm to Tokyo's efforts to curb the yen's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Traders think Bush may make such a comment possibly in exchange for a sizeable donation - possibly in billions of dollars - from Japan to help rebuild Iraq.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031014dzae001kn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
