\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers strongly, lifted by Wall St.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 14 (Reuters) - The dollar rose by one percent against the euro and the yen on Tuesday, correcting from recent lows as a rally on Wall Street encouraged investor optimism about the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has made up ground after seven consecutive weeks of losses, gaining nearly three cents against the euro from three-month lows set late last week, and putting on 1-1/2 yen from recent three-year lows against Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite Index rose to its highest close in more than 20 months on Monday after strong results from mobile phone maker Motorola Inc (MOT.N) boosted investors' hopes for strong third-quarter earnings.  \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial Average also posted its highest close since June, 2002 and U.S. stock index futures were indicating a firm open to Tuesday's Wall Street session.  \par \par \uc2 "Equity markets are supported by strong earning announcements and the U.S. is a magnet for equity capital," said Hans Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "Speculative positions in the euro have been shaken out and the bond market is declining."  \par \par \uc2 The euro has typically risen this year when bond markets have rallied, as international investors bought safe-haven euro zone debt.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to a one-week low of $1.1585 versus $1.1712 in late U.S. trade on Monday, but recovered to $1.1630 by 0745 GMT, still down 0.7 percent from the U.S. close.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also rose by one yen to 109.88 yen , then edged back to 109.65, up around 0.7 percent from late Monday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was also helped by wariness about a continuation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy levels of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's export-hurting appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose to one-week highs against the Swiss franc at 1.3357 francs , and against the Australian dollar US$0.6834 .  \par \par \uc2 Intel Corp (INTC.O), the world's largest maker of microchips, posts third quarter results later on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Germany's ZEW economic sentiment index for October is due at 0900 GMT, forecast at 63.0 compared with 60.9 in September.  \par \par \uc2 DUISENBERG STARTED IT  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's rise against the euro started on Monday after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said a Group of Seven (G7) call last month for exchange rate flexibility was not directed at the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 statement was widely perceived as a demand that Asian countries, which are largely dependent on exports, let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, but the subsequent weakness in the dollar also boosted the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the euro rose to within one cent of its record high of $1.1932 hit earlier in the year. Many traders think euro long positions had accumulated to the extent that an adjustment was inevitable.  \par \par \uc2 "People are as bullish on the dollar today as they were bearish last week," said a London-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 But some traders took Duisenberg's comments as a sign that European policymakers were unhappy with the euro's rise and were ready to rein it in.  \par \par \uc2 European Economic and Monetary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes was quoted in an Italian newspaper on Tuesday saying the euro's rise could help confirm European recovery next year and could allow easing of monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 FOCUS ON BUSH VISIT  \par \par \uc2 Traders noted uncertainty about Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ahead of Bush's visit to Japan on Friday. Bush is expected to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he meets Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi.  \par \par \uc2 If Bush preaches the virtues of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, that would be taken as indirect criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would lift the yen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Bush may say he believes in a strong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 That would be a shot in the arm for Tokyo's efforts to curb the yen's strength and traders think Bush may make such a comment possibly in exchange for a sizable donation - possibly in the billions of dollars - from Japan to help rebuild Iraq.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031014dzae000xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
