\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-Dollar creeps up, focus on Bush's trip to Asia.   \b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 03:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In TOKYO story headlined "Dollar creeps up, focus on Bush's\par  trip to Asia" please read in 6th paragraph ....108.90/00 in late\par  U.S. trade .... instead of ....109.90/00 in late U.S. trade\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (correcting comparative dollar/yen level).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 A corrected story follows.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 14 (Reuters) - The dollar crept up against the euro and the yen on Tuesday but the market lacked clear direction ahead of U.S. President George W. Bush's visit to Asia later this week.   \par \par \uc2 On Monday, the euro fell nearly one percent against the dollar after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said a Group of Seven (G7) call last month for exchange rate flexibility was not directed at the euro.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think what Mr Duisenberg said was anything new. The market's position was tilted towards the euro and so I think the fall was an adjustment," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2 As of 0045 GMT, the euro had eased to $1.1663/68 from 1.712/16 in late U.S. trade on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was feeling the pinch from selling by Japanese exporters and foreign fund operators. The euro had slipped to 127.08/11 yen compared with 127.45.   \par \par \uc2 The greenback was at 108.97/00 yen compared with 108.90/00 in late U.S. trade. On Friday, the greenback fell to a fresh three-year low of 108.30 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese markets were closed on Monday for a national holiday.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the recent dollar-selling sentiment has changed...but the dollar fell to the 108 yen level and I think there is a sense of achievement in the market for now," said Tanase.   \par \par \uc2 The G7 issued a statement in September calling for more flexibility in exchange rates. That was widely perceived in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a demand that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of their export markets.   \par \par \uc2 Players were shifting their focus to Bush's visit to Japan on Friday, but few expect any surprises.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a topic at the meeting and talks will be about Iraq," said a trader at a Japanese bank. "But since many people are focusing on the meeting. I will also keep an eye out."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031014dzae000nh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
