\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Mixed With Tokyo Mkts Closed For Holiday  \b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2003\par 09:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The dollar was mixed against its major counterparts during Asian hours in holiday-thinned trading.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar weakened slightly against the yen, while gaining modestly against the euro and sterling. Trading volumes were light because financial markets in Tokyo were closed for a public holiday.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT the dollar was quoted at Y108.46, down from Y108.62 late Friday in New York and Y109.10 late Friday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1758, down from $1.1805 late Friday in New York, but up from $1.1737 late Friday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was quoted at Y127.44, down from Y128.20 late Friday in New York and Y128.05 late Friday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar could see further declines against the yen this week if U.S. President George W. Bush's visit to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  causes Japanese authorities to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cap the yen's rise, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration, at the behest of U.S. manufacturers, has been arguing that market forces - not government policy-makers - should determine the appropriate level of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The manufacturers claim that manipulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Asian governments has kept the dollar artificially high, thereby hurting the cost competitiveness of U.S. goods in global markets.  \par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to be a major focus of Bush's talks with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, "the Bank of Japan may be reluctant to make a conspicuous show of force in the market ahead of the meetings," UBS said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via the BOJ, has been in the market buying vast amounts of U.S. dollars this year in an attempt to cap the yen's rise and preserve the pricing power of the country's exporters. The MOF has spent Y13.5 trillion this year - including a record Y4.46 trillion in September alone - to tame the surging yen.  \par \par \uc2 If the MOF is exercising a bit of restraint ahead of Bush's weeklong trip to Asia, so too is the U.S. Treasury Department.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury has delayed the release of a Treasury Department semiannual report to Congress on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by U.S. trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 "The delay is believed to accommodate President Bush, who would be seeking moral, financial and logistical support for Iraq from its key Asian allies," DBS Bank said in a note to clients.  \par \par \uc2 -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ryan Woo  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031013dzad00087\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
