\par\par \b \uc2 Debt Derivatives Diary: On A Temporary  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Watch  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristina Zurla  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2003\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO (Dow Jones)--Participants in credit futures markets have been closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the lookout for Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the U.S. dollar hovers at a three-year low against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 That has provided some support to the Treasury market and may continue to do so following the holiday break Monday, but participants say the intermediate trend is still toward lower prices and higher cash yields in Treasury bonds and notes as the economic data begins to heat up.  \par \par \uc2 A 23,000 drop in weekly claims for state unemployment insurance caused the December bond futures contract to sink more than a point Thursday, but talk of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would prompt the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Treasurys lent some support to the market and kept both bonds and notes basically in a range last week.  \par \par \uc2 There was talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  all week, but Friday it began to heat up after the BOJ said it was easing the limit on its account balance target, in a move that could facilitate market action.  \par \par \uc2 "Their (BOJ) buying has had an enormous impact on the bounce in Treasurys, and on holiday-lightened trading days people are always afraid they will come in," said Gary Hynes, a broker with TJM Futures. "They certainly don't mind spending money supporting the dollar, and no question, that money is funneled into Treasurys."  \par \par \uc2 While there was word of Japanese buying all week in the market, participants say it hasn't been that large a push. Even if there has been buying, the BOJ hasn't been all that successful at defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the yen hasn't seen a significant downward correction.  \par \par \uc2 In the latest week, the Fed's so-called custody holdings of Treasury and agency securities held by the Fed on behalf of foreign official institutions rose $18.2 billion. The purchases likely include investments of dollars bought by the BOJ in its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts.  \par \par \uc2 "With the dollar continuing to be weak, some are concluding that the BOJ will likely remain a buyer of Treasurys," said Anthony Crescenzi, an analyst with Miller Tabak & Co.  \par \par \uc2 He said there is a feeling of that given the aforementioned evidence of foreign participation in the market, there could be more coming. "The BOJ might continue to be an aggressive buyer given continued weakness of the dollar against the yen," he said. The dollar/yen is sitting near a zone where \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been done in the past, hovering above 108.00.  \par \par \uc2 While that may keep Treasurys temporarily afloat, in the end, the market doesn't take its cue from the BOJ, but rather, the Federal Reserve and the economy, said Crescenzi.  \par \par \uc2 "It's lending support, but I'd call it only temporary support," he said. "We have a heavy calendar of economic reports coming up and strength in the data will have a greater impact on Treasury price action."  \par \par \uc2 As to when the BOJ might come into the market again, some say it could be near midweek.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen keeps pushing to new high ground. President Bush will be traveling to Asia next week and will meet with the Japanese, among others," said Lou Brien, a strategist with DRW Trading Group. He said as a result, it appears Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is laying low for now ahead of the meetings.  \par \par \uc2 Given a Monday holiday in both the U.S. and Japan, one Eurodollar futures broker at the Chicago Mercantile Exchange speculated that "the best timing will likely be Wednesday morning." He said if they come in, short-term interest rate futures markets such as the Eurodollar will likely also see a temporary spike that will quickly be sold into once that factor is out of the way and the market turns back to watching U.S. economic data.  \par \par \uc2 Eurodollar futures have been trapped in a very tight range, and are indicating no monetary policy changes from the U.S. Federal Reserve for the rest of the year and well into 2004.  \par \par \uc2 After the Monday Columbus Day holiday break in the U.S., the data calendar kicks off Tuesday with the Richmond Federal Reserve survey, followed by what is likely to be the highlight of the week Wednesday - September retail sales data. Thursday and Friday's slate of reports include industrial production, the consumer price index, and consumer sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 Participants in credit futures will also keep a watch on equities, as the third-quarter earnings reporting season heats up and outlooks from companies will also help confirm whether the economy really does seem to be improving.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                 Week of Oct 6         Week of Oct 13 \par                 Low       High      Support   Resistance \par Dec T-bonds    106-28    109-21      106-28    112-08 \par Dec 10-Yrs     110-17   112-31+     109-20+   114-24 \par Mar Eurodlr    98.725    98.775      98.710    98.805 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kristina Zurla, Dow Jones Newswires; 312-750-4132; kristina.zurla@dowjones.com.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1065 4095  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031013dzad0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
