\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro falls after Duisenberg comments.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2003\par 21:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 13 (Reuters) - The euro fell against the dollar and yen on Monday after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said a Group of Seven call for exchange rate flexibility last month was not directed at the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "There was no statement specifically about euro/dollar, that was maybe misunderstood," Duisenberg told reporters on the sidelines of a news conference in Moscow.  \par \par \uc2 Duisenberg's attempt to clarify why the dollar has fallen so far and so fast against the euro seemed to make thin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets dispel dollar-bearish sentiment, for now.  \par \par \uc2 In September, the G7 issued a statement calling for more flexibility in exchange rates. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market widely perceived this as a demand that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of their export markets. The call pushed the yen to three-year highs against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, swept up in a frenzy of dollar sales, earlier this month had risen to within 1 cent of its record high of $1.1932 hit earlier in the year.  \par \par \uc2 But Duisenberg's clarification on Monday sent the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down more than 1 percent against the dollar and the yen. The euro last week had vaulted to its highest since mid-June against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In late Monday afternoon New York trade, the euro was down 0.9 percent to $1.1692.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro declined 0.6 percent against the yen  to 127.40 yen. Against\par sterling          , the euro fell more than 1 percent to 70.11 pence.\par The dollar rose 0.3 percent against the yen  to 108.93 yen. Against the\par Swiss franc        the dollar was at 1.3241 francs, up 0.9 percent on\par the day. The pound        was up 0.1 percent at $1.6668.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Today's comments are back on messages, noting as several European officials have said in recent days, that the G7 statement was aimed at Asia, not Europe," said Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist HSBC in New York. "This has helped push the euro to new five-week lows against the yen."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. dealings were marked by a holiday, a lack of economic news or data and scant trading volumes, all of which aided the dollar's recovery, amid profit-taking in euros, some New York based traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro slipped through a support level near $1.1740, then triggered a series of stop loss orders on the way down to session lows around $1.1654, before making a partial recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It turned into a stop-loss fest in illiquid conditions," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, New Jersey. "But I think the (euro's fall to around) $1.1650 was an overshoot," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Later in the week, markets could react in anticipation of a raft of U.S. economic data and corporate earnings, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some analysts noted recent gains in U.S. stocks as a contributor to\par euro weakness and dollar strength. The Dow Jones industrial average\par was up 0.7 percent and the Nasdaq composite index         was up 0.6\par percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chicago Federal Reserve President Michael Moskow said in a television interview on CNBC that the U.S. economy needs to grow quickly for an extended period to make inroads into the unemployment rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. President George W. Bush visits Asia this week and next. His first stop will be Japan on Friday. He heads to Bangkok on Saturday for an Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation meeting on October 20-21.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talk circulated that Bush would press Chinese President Hu Jintao to revalue the yuan when they meet in Bangkok. That kept a focus on the dollar adjustment, though no immediate action was expected to result. (Additional reporting by Kyle Peterson in Chicago and Charlotte Cooper in London).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031014dzad000nf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
