\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro tumbles after Duisenberg comments.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2003\par 17:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Oct 13 (Reuters) - The euro tumbled against the dollar and yen on Monday after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said a Group of Seven call for exchange rate flexibility last month was not directed at the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "There was no statement specifically about euro/dollar, that was maybe misunderstood," Duisenberg told reporters on the sidelines of a news conference in Moscow.  \par \par \uc2 Duisenberg's attempt to clarify why the dollar has fallen so far and so fast against the euro seemed to make thin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets reconsider dollar-bearish sentiments.  \par \par \uc2 In September, the G7 issued a statement calling for more flexibility in exchange rates. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market widely perceived this as a demand that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of their export markets. The call pushed the yen to three-year highs against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, swept up in a frenzy of dollar sales, meanwhile, rose to within one cent of its all-time high.  \par \par \uc2 Duisenberg's clarification on Monday sent the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down more than 1 percent against the dollar and the yen. The euro last week had vaulted to its highest since mid-June against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Today's comments are back on messages, noting as several European officials have said in recent days, that the G7 statement was aimed at Asia, not Europe," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist HSBC in New York. "This has helped push the euro to new five-week lows against the yen."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In New York mid-morning trade, the euro fell 0.73 percent to $1.1709\par and was down 0.82 percent at 127.10 yen          . The dollar edged up\par 0.23 percent to 108.87 yen        and was up 0.76 percent at 1.3232\par Swiss francs       . Sterling was near flat at $1.6653       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. dealings were muted by a holiday and a lack of economic news or data. But later in the week, markets could react in anticipation of a raft of U.S. economic data and corporate earnings.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some analysts noted recent gains in U.S. stocks as a contributor to\par euro weakness and dollar strength. The Dow Jones industrial average\par was up 1.03 percent and the Nasdaq composite index         was up 0.98\par percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The stock market is healthy," said Tim Mazanec, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "Maybe there are more believers today than there were yesterday."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chicago Federal Reserve President Michael Moskow said in a television interview on CNBC that the U.S. economy needs to grow quickly for an extended period to make inroads into the unemployment rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. President George W. Bush visits Asia this week and next. His first stop will be Japan on Friday. He heads to Bangkok on Saturday for an Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation meeting on October 20-21.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talk circulated that Bush would press Chinese President Hu Jintao to revalue the yuan when they meet in Bangkok. That kept a focus on the dollar adjustment, though no immediate action was expected to result.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I would think generally this is not going to be a great week for the dollar again, even though we're going to see some good releases both on the supply and demand side in the U.S.," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It still seems to me markets are ignoring or have fully discounted this cyclical recovery in the U.S. and are now much more focused back on the structural negatives that remain a key burden on the dollar," he said. (Additional reporting by Charlotte Cooper in LONDON).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031013dzad002u3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
