\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips vs yen - Japan optimism rises.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2003\par 17:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 10 (Reuters) - The dollar slid against the yen on Friday as speculators bought the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a belief that the latest Bank of Japan monetary easing will help bolster recovery of the world's second-biggest economy.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also took a beating from other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after rallying on Thursday on fairly buoyant U.S. jobs data. Sentiment on the dollar was turning bearish again amid a growing perception that it has to weaken to help finance the ballooning U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a speculative frost out there. Thematically, it is a continuation of the dollar's downtrend," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast Inc in New York.  \par \par \uc2 In midday New York trade, the euro climbed 0.48 percent to $1.1798. The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also rose 0.05 percent against the yen to 127.97 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3116 francs, down 0.37 percent on the day. The pound, meanwhile, fell slightly against the dollar to $1.6647.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar's rally on Thursday after upbeat weekly U.S. jobless claims served to clear out short positions on the greenback, specifically against the euro, suggesting further weakness in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 BOJ SURPRISE EASING  \par \par \uc2 But the story of the day was the BoJ's surprise easing overnight. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly raised the upper end of its target for banking system liquidity and made its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped 0.61 percent to 108.41 yen, after falling to a new three-year low of 108.28. Traders remained alert to signs of possible BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although some analysts believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step aside ahead of U.S. President George W. Bush's visit to Tokyo next week.  \par \par \uc2 "The market completely shrugged off the BoJ's move. The dollar/yen is still a one-way bet in favor of further dollar weakness," said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York.  \par \par \uc2 BoJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui said the easing was intended to underpin a nascent economic recovery and Tokyo's Nikkei average responded positively, ending up 2.42 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Aiming to boost excess liquidity in the banking system, the BoJ said it would raise the upper end of its target range for current account deposits that banks keep at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 32 trillion yen ($293 billion) from 30 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The easing is helpful but it would just be on the margin. I don't think the measure would make a difference in stemming the yen's appreciation," said 4Cast's Ruskin.  \par \par \uc2 Other analysts said the yen's advance in the face of easing suggests the market is not taking the BoJ seriously.  \par \par \uc2 "There seems to be a loss of credibility on the part of the BoJ," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York. "We continue to think that it becomes harder to defend any line-in-the-sand figure," she added.  \par \par \uc2 US TRADE, PPI DATA  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gained a modest, fleeting bid against the euro after the U.S. trade deficit narrowed unexpectedly in August to $39.2 billion, down from a revised tally of $40 billion in July.  \par \par \uc2 "The trade numbers are better both in real and nominal terms. It tells you that trade won't be a drag in the third quarter," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The September U.S. producer price index rose to a faster-than-expected pace of 0.3 percent, compared to a rise of 0.4 percent in August. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York and Natsuko Waki in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031010dzaa002vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
