\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Finishes Lower in Muted Pre-Holiday Trading  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Flint and Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2003\par 22:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar slipped lower Friday, giving back day-earlier gains against its European counterparts as activity wound down ahead of what will be a three-day weekend for many market participants.  \par \par \uc2 Most New York \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks will be lightly manned Monday as the Columbus Day federal holiday in the U.S. and national holidays in Canada and Japan will likely keep volume thin and trading muted.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar received only a short-lived boost from an unexpected narrowing of the U.S. trade gap announced at the beginning of the New York session. Earlier in global trading, the U.S.\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had resumed its downtrend after rallying Thursday on the back of a decline in weekly U.S. jobless claims. For the second day in a row, the euro came within a cent of its all-time peak at $1.1934 reached in May, topping out at $1.1851.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar recovered a bit later in the morning session, partly attributable to last-minute adjustments of positions as well as profit-taking, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements slowing almost to a standstill by midday.  \par \par \uc2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.1805, up from $1.1740 late Thursday. The dollar was trading at 108.62 yen, from 109.11 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3117 franc, below 1.3235 franc Thursday. The pound was at $1.6650, little changed from $1.6658.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. trade shortfall contracted for the fifth straight month in August to $39.2 billion, after narrowing to $40.3 billion in July. Expectations were for a widening to $41.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the U.S. trade numbers contrasted sharply with the rising trade and current account surpluses in Japan. On Thursday, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that the country's trade surplus had grown 20.3% on the year, while the broader current account surplus was up 33.6%.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. trade gap has come into sharper focus for the market following last month's call by Group of Seven finance ministers for greater flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in an attempt to correct global imbalances. The huge U.S. current account deficit requires capital inflows from overseas to help finance it, lest the dollar would fall.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. producer price index rose 0.3% in September, slowing from a 0.4% rate in August. The closely watched core index, which excludes food and energy items, was unchanged after a 0.1% gain in August.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations were for an increase of 0.1% in both the core and overall indexes in September.  \par \par \uc2 "The trade numbers were OK," said David Leaver, senior dealer at Gain Capital, but he added that longer-term investors continue to sell into dollar rallies.  \par \par \uc2 And investors continued to keep an eye out for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though there haven't been any convincing signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Thursday, when Japanese authorities were suspected of buying up dollars several times during the day.  \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Flint,Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031010dzaa0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
