\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Of Texans And Lines In The Sand  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,126 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2003\par 17:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Robert Flint \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--If any more lines are drawn in the sand regarding the dollar's decline, it may be President George W. Bush who does the drawing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Japanese monetary authorities apparently no longer have a point beyond which the yen won't be allowed to appreciate. Although traders are convinced that covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowed the greenback's slide, nice round numbers like Y110 or Y115 don't appear to have much significance.  \par \par \uc2 After spending an estimated $100 billion this year through mid-September to keep the dollar above Y115, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently abandoned an active defense of its long-standing line in the sand that was widely regarded as the break-even dollar rate for the country's exporters.  \par \par \uc2 But that doesn't mean anything goes. As long as the dollar descends in an orderly fashion, Japan is willing to let it drop. Should the pace become so rapid the dollar risks tumbling into free fall, the MOF stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the BOJ.  \par \par \uc2 Maintaining two-way risk in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is also an important factor. The communique published after the Dubai meeting of the Group of Seven leading nations created some confusion because many market participants interpreted the call for more flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates as an invitation to sell the dollar. If everyone is selling, the dollar can only go in one direct - down, very fast.  \par \par \uc2 Even before the G7 communique was released, investors of all stripes had begun unloading the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the dollar zoomed through the once-sancrosanct Y115 level faster than spit through a trumpet. Just ten days after the G7 issued the flexibility communique, the dollar was threatening Y110 and below. At that point, the MOF showed the world it still had the stomach and muscle to fight a delaying action. Through its accounts with the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets on Sept. 30.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Y110 level soon gave way under renewed pressure, and the BOJ was back in markets again this week, acting covertly through agent banks to slow the dollar's slide. Friday morning, the dollar was trading Y108.49  \par \par \uc2 "Most observers now seem to recognize that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at smoothing the yen's appreciation rather than arresting it," wrote Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York, in an e-mail research note.  \par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of the Dubai meeting, financial authorities in Asia, Europe and the U.S. have endeavored to put some nuances in the "flexibility means weaker dollar" interpretation of the G7 communique. A gradual downward correction for the dollar is desirable, but tendencies that suggest a sudden meltdown will meet with stiff resistance, seems to be the real message.  \par \par \uc2 Reassurances Wednesday from a White House spokesman and Thursday from Treasury Secretary John Snow that the U.S. hadn't abandoned the strong dollar policy are probably also intended to soothe investors left jittery by developments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  scene.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Treading A Dangerous Path \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's a dangerous path that Snow and his counterparts have to tread and the market will punish any missteps. Nearly everyone recognizes that the imbalances in the world economy caused by the huge U.S. trade and current account deficits can no longer be ignored. The obvious solution is a long-term decline in the dollar's value, but that's by no means easy to engineer. Should investors from abroad dump dollar-denominated assets in panic, economic recovery could be threatened in all parts of the world.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same time, it's obvious the current U.S. administration is happy to see the dollar decline, if for no other reason than to head off criticism from political opponents. The U.S. won't renounce the strong dollar policy, but it won't stand in the way of market forces pushing the dollar down.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The strong dollar policy is not what it was and that's why the market is not going to take much notice of these sorts of comments," said Steve Barrow, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bear Stearns International Ltd. in London in an e-mail update.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets are likely to stay nervous at least through the Bush trip to Asia and the Oct. 20-21 meeting of the heads of governments of the Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation in Thailand. Snow's testimony on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation before the Senate Banking Committee, which had been scheduled for Oct. 16 but has now been postponed, will in any event garner far more attention that the annual Treasury report on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually does.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We expect some speculation that the reason for the delay of Snow's speech is that the U.S. administration is more pleased with (dollar)-Asia developments since the G7 meeting," wrote the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  team of ABN-Amro in a research note. "However, given that Bush is in Asia next week, fresh commentary calling for exchange rate adjustments is still on the cards."  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading is likely to remain volatile until investors get a convincing combination of words and actions from not only the G7's leaders but China's as well. Since the U.S. turned up the heat under the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the Bush administration will also have to play a leading roll in preventing it from boiling over and scalding the world economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Like Baby Bear's porridge in Goldilocks, the dollar's level has to be just right - it can't be too strong or too weak. Given the skepticism of investors, the tendency of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to overshoot and the waning effect of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  whether verbal, covert or open, it may fall to Bush at some point in the near future to draw a new line in the sand for the dollar's defense.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That brings to mind another famous Texan who drew a line in the sand - William Barret Travis at the Alamo in 1836. Although part of the Texas creation myth and a great testimony to both courage and dedication to duty, the legend of the Travis line carries some very unfortunate connotations for any commander planning a defense. After an intense struggle, the Alamo's defenders were wiped out within 45 minutes of the start of the final attack.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Robert Flint has covered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past five years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Robert Flint, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1141 4137  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031010dzaa00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
