\par\par \b \uc2 Five Year Tsy Auction Spurs Debate Over Central Bk Role  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael S. Derby  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2003\par 15:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A very well bid Treasury five-year note auction held on Wednesday has market participants wondering just who did all the unexpected buying.  \par \par \uc2 The leading view among many bond traders is that it was foreign central banks, namely the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who have dollars to burn from their recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They point to a key statistic released by the Treasury after the auction, which showed half of the bidding came from "indirect" bidders, doubling what was seen in the previous five-year note sale.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury defines such bidders as "customers placing competitive bids through a direct submitter, including foreign and international monetary authorities," the latter of which are central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Those who believe such institutions bought so heavily in the sale welcomed the move, hoping it signals that they will be a continued source of support in a market under the gun from improving economic news that diminishes the attractiveness of government bonds.  \par \par \uc2 But skeptics abound. They argue the Treasury data are simply not specific enough to conclude who did the buying. They add there are many reasons why all kinds of participants, namely large investment funds, may have wanted to buy that specific five year note, and that no one should be making any assumptions about future sources of support for the market.  \par \par \uc2 Still, many are confident it was the central banks that did the buying. The fear central banks may be fading as a force in the market goes back to the most recent meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading nations that appeared to point to diminished \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such a policy shift would reduce the amount of dollars available to foreign central banks for Treasury purchases.  \par \par \uc2 After the G7 meeting, "the fear among bond traders...is that the demand for U.S. Treasurys from central banks would not be as strong going forward," said Edward Rydwelski, strategist with Briefing.com, in Chicago. The five year note sale "tells us demand for Treasurys from foreign investors remains strong. It's certainly not attenuating," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan is seen by many as the most likely candidate for these types of purchases. It has acknowledged one recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen by buying dollars, and there have been numerous signs it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on other occasions.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve data showed a $16.9 billion rise in the level of securities it holds in custody for central banks in the most recent week, a very large jump that was clearly driven by unusual forces. The week before, Treasury custody holdings were up $6.9 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "The logical candidate" for the increase in foreign holdings and the five-year note outcome is Japan, said William Sullivan, an economist with Morgan Stanley in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Given the nascent signs of recovery there and the importance cheap exports play in that process, "the Japanese are not in a position to let the yen appreciate significantly" and are likely to keep buying dollars, which will then make their way into the Treasury market and support prices, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Statistical Noise \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But as certain as many are that central banks powered the hot five year note sale, others aren't so sure. The paramount criticism rests on the fact that the Treasury's statistics aren't precise enough to allow anyone to reach a definitive conclusion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are a lot of conspiracy theories going on here," said Richard Gilhooly, market strategist with BNP Paribas in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He doubts central banks were as involved as many in the market believe, in part because the Treasury's sale Thursday of inflation-indexed notes also saw a robust indirect bidder presence, and central banks aren't historically known for their appetite for that type of security.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jim Bianco, of Bianco Research LLC in Chicago, said he considers much of the statistical foundation driving the debate somewhat meaningless. Perhaps more importantly, given what's happened in the past, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying factor probably isn't as important as the market seems to think.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is a school of thought that you tend to get your good auctions in bad markets, and you get your sloppy auctions in good markets," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's because when Treasurys are doing well, investors will usually just do their buying in the open market and leave auctions to the dealers who must bid in them, he said. Conversely, when the market is under pressure - Treasurys have been suffering from good economic data of late - market participants will use auctions to cover short positions, which are bets the market will go lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With no shortage of disagreement over the degree to which central banks are, and are likely to, support the market, the one thing participants can agree on is that the truly dominant force in the market will be the economic landscape. Central banks can be aggressive buyers of Treasurys in the wake of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market manipulations, but better economic numbers mean yields will suffer. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying could blunt those declines, but only by so much.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economic data will trump any sort of relative optimism relative to foreign demand for Treasurys," Briefing.com's Rydwelski said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Michael S. Derby, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4192; michael.derby@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031010dzaa0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
