\par\par \b \uc2 Rapid yen rally expected to start losing momentum.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Mair  \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2003\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 9 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen has rallied eight percent to three-year highs in less than a month, but analysts expect its momentum will taper off as authorities resist further gains and China keeps the yuan steady.  \par \par \uc2 The yen charged to three-year highs around 108.90 per dollar on Thursday, and while momentum could see it push on further, 105 per dollar is expected to prove the limit of its ambitions for this year.  \par \par \uc2 Global impatience with Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and signs of economic growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have driven the rally that last month saw the yen snap the crucial 115 per dollar level.  \par \par \uc2 "It's obviously moved very quickly in the last couple of weeks, and I think they are concerned about the pace of it," Patrick Bennett, Commerzbank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Singapore, said of the Japanese authorities reaction to the yen's surge.  \par \par \uc2 "Really what they want to do is get people attuned to what will be a stronger yen, give people time to adjust."  \par \par \uc2 Bennett, who does not think there is a "line in the sand" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength that authorities look to defend, said the yen could rise to 105 yen by the end of 2003, and 100 per dollar, last traded in November 1995, was a realistic target next year.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have stepped up their rhetoric to stem the yen's gains, and there has been overt as well as suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 "While the improved economic outlook is likely to keep the underlying flows very supportive of the yen, we expect the Japanese authorities to remain a presence in the market, attempting to slow the move," UBS strategist Bhanu Baweja said.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of over 50 analysts found that the yen was expected to hold around 110 yen over the next six months, and then rise to 105 yen by the end of September next year.  \par \par \uc2 That would be a gain of about four percent over the next year from Thursday's highs, about half the gains since September 15.  \par \par \uc2 "The pressure will probably ease back off dollar/yen, at least in the short term, but the general direction is down towards 105 next year," said Jo Masters, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Macquarie Bank in Sydney.  \par \par \uc2 G7 SPARK, CHINA EYED  \par \par \uc2 The spark for the yen's rise was the Group of Seven's September 20 call for greater flexibility in exchange rates to share the cost of adjusting to the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The yen actually started rallying from around 117.70 per dollar on September 15, as the market bet that since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a topic at the G7 summit, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not invite extra criticism by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the meeting.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governing council member Ernst Welteke spelt out this week that the G7 wanted Asia to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising against the dollar, rather than engineer a broadly weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "It was aimed at the Asian imbalances with the United States and it was not aimed at the euro," Welteke told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 But the yen's surge has not been matched by its neighbours - only one, the Thai baht, has risen by four percent since mid-September.  \par \par \uc2 Perhaps most importantly, despite increasing market speculation, China has not revalued its yuan, which it manages in a range of 8.2760/8.2800 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts do not expect China to make any change in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the near term, and even in 2004 the moves are seen as more token than significant.  \par \par \uc2 That will keep Asian central banks active to protect their key export markts, and make Japan wary of the yen rallying too hard.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as China pegs the renminbi to the U.S. dollar it makes perfect sense to us for other central banks to do the same - unless their governments are prepared to lose export competitiveness both in the United States and in China," Michael Spencer, Deutsche Bank's chief Asia economist, wrote in a report.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031009dza90016q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
