\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar broadly weak, plumbs 3-year lows vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2003\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Shinichi Kishima\par  TOKYO, Oct 9 (Reuters) - The dollar plumbed three-year lows\par  against the yen and 3-=   month lows versus the euro on Thursday\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 amid growing views the U.S. and European authorities would let it weaken to correct a ballooning U.S. current account deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Although the Japanese officials have made no secret they were miffed by the yen's recent rise, their resolve to keep it in check was being tested by a market emboldened by an apparent laissez-faire stance by those overseeing other major economies.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar briefly spiked up against the yen from its troughs on what traders said might be Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the bounce was modest and quickly invited fresh selling.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) officials were not immediately available to confirm they had stepped into the market.  \par \par \uc2 "If it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the limited bounce looks rather sad. It if was not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the absence of such an action even at these levels is in itself a bearish message," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Either way, it makes it easier for people to sell dollars."  \par \par \uc2 At 0525 GMT, the dollar was stuck near three-year lows around 109.10 yen it set earlier in the day, down a half yen on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to around $1.1850 its highest level in about 3-= months and within a cent of record highs around $1.1930 set in June.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar's bounces from lows have been getting smaller by the day this week as Japanese exporters abandon hopes of a trend reversal and lower their sell orders.  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese exporters have not been able to catch up with the dollar's rapid decline and their need to sell dollars will cap any significant rebound," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 STRONG DOLLAR POLICY  \par \par \uc2 The White House said on Wednesday that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was still that of a strong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But traders said the esoteric mantra rang hollow, particularly after recent remarks by President George W. Bush that he wanted other countries to adopt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy that offered a "level playing field" for American companies.  \par \par \uc2 Combined with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's comments that the huge U.S. current account deficit made a weaker dollar "inevitable", Bush's words were taken as a signal for traders to sell the greenback. Traders said speculation about U.S. policy would likely continue to dominate the market.  \par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the U.S. Senate Banking Committee on Wednesday confirmed that Treasury Secretary John Snow's testimony on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been delayed so the panel can consider an overhaul of mortgage finance regulation.  \par \par \uc2 Policies aside, fund flows were pointing to continued yen strength.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data on Thursday showed that foreigners were net buyers of Japanese equities to the tune of 1.0 trillion yen in September, down from 1.6 trillion yen in August but still huge.  \par \par \uc2 Separate MOF data showed that Japan's current account surplus widened by 33.6 percent in August from a year earlier, with the trade surplus growing by 20.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 But economists said the fallout from the yen's strength would probably start affecting Japan's trade in the coming months, given the dollar had actually begun its decline at the start of last year from levels around 135 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we are coming to a stage where the yen's strength will start to take its toll," said Koichi Ono, economist at Daiwa Institute of Research.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen has already strengthened significantly and may continue to do so for some time."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031009dza9000uj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
