\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Sets New 3-Yr Low Vs Yen, But Y109 Holds  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Twaronite  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2003\par 07:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar remained under pressure in Asia Thursday, falling to a new three-year low as traders awaited clear signs of yen-selling by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A nervous market watched the dollar tick below Tuesday's Y109.35 three-year low a few minutes after the Tokyo lunch hour began at 0300 GMT. Dollar sellers were said to have included a Japanese bank known to have been a conduit for official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past.  \par \par \uc2 But the break of the old low was not so significant, market participants and observers said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar "has bounced off the 109.30's three times, so this is just a breakdown through that," said J.P. Morgan strategist James Malcolm.  \par \par \uc2 He and other traders said the real level to watch was the Y109 options barrier. A drop though that support would likely trigger stop-loss orders and set the pair up for a rapid drop to Y108.80.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar came within 10 ticks of that support, establishing a new three-year low of Y109.10, before suddenly rebounding to near Y109.40. The move put already nervous market participants on red alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to stem the yen's gains.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is so jumpy that every rise starts rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader at a European bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 With so many participants apparently gearing up for a test of the downside, he said, the dollar will likely try the Y109 level later this session, unless clear signs of MOF activity emerge.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y109.17, below Y109.55 in New York and Y109.66 in Tokyo late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1837, higher than $1.1816 in New York and $1.1784 in Tokyo. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y129.22, compared with Y129.47 in New York and Y129.22 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6665, compared with $1.6625 in New York and $1.6647 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Adding to pressure on the dollar Thursday were Japanese reports, including one in the Nihon Keizai Shimbun, that U.S. President George W. Bush will discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters with Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi. The two will meet at the end of next week when Bush comes to Tokyo Oct. 17-18.  \par \par \uc2 "Certainly they've got to talk about it, so this in itself isn't much of a reason for selling" the dollar, a U.S. brokerage trader said.  \par \par \uc2 But any hint that the U.S. is leaning on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity - and any subsequent indication that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might respond to such pressure - is "sure to be taken as a green light to sell dollars," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters Thursday morning that the dollar's recent decline against not only the yen but the euro as well was likely to correct eventually, given the underlying strength of the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 "When markets move in one direction, they eventually come back," Mizoguchi said.  \par \par \uc2 Some market observers have also suggested that the Bank of Japan's current Y30-trillion account balance ceiling may be curbing Tokyo's ability to conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A big surplus makes it hard for the BOJ to keep its balance under Y30 trillion if the MOF wants to embark on more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and this could be a topic at the BOJ's policy board meeting Thursday and Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Therefore, if the BOJ were to hike its balance target above Y30 trillion, or issue a statement that it could do so in the future, market participants might take that as a sign that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facilitating more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also lost a little ground to the euro Thursday, extending its new four-month high of $1.1840 hit overnight to $1.1850.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro traded between Y129.06 and Y129.65, within overnight ranges, as Japanese exporters and European fund sales kept that pair near the bottom of its range for most of the Asian afternoon session.  \par \par \uc2 -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie and John Gibson  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031009dza90005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
