\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips, dogged by current account deficit.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 22:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped against most other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday amid a growing belief the greenback will have to weaken to narrow the U.S. current account deficit and that a dollar fall has implicit official sanction, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "One thing is pretty clear. We're back to talking about the current account deficit," said Philip Capone, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Fortis Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 David Gilmore, a partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Conn., said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is at an "inflection point" where the focus is less on economic growth than on the U.S. current account, a broad measure of a nation's global trade.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also remained on alert for yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Japanese officials continued their jawboning overnight against the yen's rapid movements.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar came under some pressure late in the New York session as\par U.S. stocks retreated, traders said. The Dow Jones industrial average\par retreated 0.3 percent and the Nasdaq composite         was down 0.7\par percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nasdaq's slip gave the euro the impetus to push higher above the $1.18 area, said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, N.J.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Real money continues to buy the euro on dips. Sentiment in general is still dollar bearish," Leaver said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late in the New York afternoon, the euro was up 0.3 percent to $1.1801, after rising to within 1 cent of its record high earlier in the session.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the yen , the dollar fell 0.3 percent to 109.53 yen. Against\par the Swiss franc  the dollar was at 1.3138 francs, down 0.2 percent on\par the day. The pound        was virtually flat at $1.6619.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gilmore said there is broad acceptance in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market that both the United States and Europe are resigned to a weaker dollar. "I think there is broad recognition in the market that there is not much to the U.S. government's strong dollar policy," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, however, the White House said it is still supporting a strong dollar despite recent comments by both European and U.S. officials that traders have viewed as an attempt to encourage a fall in the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our policy has always been one of a strong dollar policy," White House spokesman Scott McClellan told reporters in response to a question.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The dollar didn't get help versus the euro on the strong dollar comments. If the administration continues to reiterate it during dollar weakness, that blunts the policy," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But comments from European officials this week led by outgoing European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg that a dollar fall was "unavoidable" have encouraged traders to probe levels for the euro above $1.18. Traders said the euro seems to be a one-way bet against the dollar and as long as short-term moves above $1.17 hold, the uptrend remains in place.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were also nervously awaiting U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's appearance at a Senate hearing on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies next week, and a summit meeting of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in Bangkok later this month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STILL ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WATCH  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders remained alert for any possible BoJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  following more yen-related comments from Japanese officials overnight.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fierce yen-weakening rhetoric ensued in Asian trading, with Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki indicating that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on sharp yen moves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031008dza8003eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
