\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Mizoguchi says dollar fall caused by speculation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 03:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 8 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Wednesday that a broad-based decline in the dollar was caused by speculative movements.   \par \par \uc2  "There are speculative movements aiming to push the dollar lower against the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters.   \par \par \uc2  He added he thought the euro was stabilising a bit.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remained unchanged and Tokyo would take action as needed, he said, although he declined to comment on suspected yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets overnight when the dollar fell below 110 yen.   \par \par \uc2  "Stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is important. We will continue to closely monitor the market and take appropriate action as necessary." The dollar was at 109.57/60 yen in early Tokyo trade, just shy of three-year lows around 109.35 yen hit overnight. Traders suspected Japanese authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight after the dollar fell below the key psychological level of 110 yen.   \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said the U.S. economy remained strong but that markets held a biased view, which was also prompting speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  "The market is focusing on the U.S. current account deficit but that is not an issue that needs to be urgently addressed. We don't want to see a sudden adjustment in (the deficit), nor does the Group of Seven intend that," he said.   \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. economy will remain strong over the medium term and markets should reflect that."   \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi declined to comment on remarks by Economics and Financial Services Minister Heizo Takenaka who said late on Tuesday that the yen's recent rise was partly due to fund flows into Japanese assets.   \par \par \uc2  But he said recent comments by various officials, such as European ones, were prompting speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  The euro's climb against the dollar accelerated after outgoing European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said earlier this week that the dollar's decline was "unavoidable".   \par \par \uc2  Asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, Mizoguchi said that, as usual, the Finance Ministry would disclose monthly and quarterly data on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The exception would be if Tokyo used foreign central banks as its agent, in which case it would immediately confirm such action to avoid confusion about the source of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031008dza80004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
