\par\par \b \uc2 ASSET CLASS: The  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Conundrum  \b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 12:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Alen Mattich \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets are proving a big headache for investors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  everyone wants to play is off-limits, the one that's least attractive no one can quite do without, while everything else is beset with problems.  \par \par \uc2 Most investors right now would love to get hold of the Chinese renminbi, or yuan.  \par \par \uc2 With the Chinese economy growing somewhere north of 8% a year and exports booming amid vast untapped potential, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks a sure bet far into the future.  \par \par \uc2 But that's an idle dream. Not only is the yuan fixed against the U.S. dollar, but it's not convertible.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese government doesn't want the renminbi to appreciate against its biggest trade partner, so it's not letting anyone in on the game. Market participants have tried to get around the stricture by using the futures market, but that's a half measure at best and doesn't give access to Chinese assets.  \par \par \uc2 A shame really. Were it to be released from its mooring lines, the yuan would rise from its peg of some 8.3 to the dollar to around 5 in fairly short order, a 40% appreciation, and then would probably head higher still, figures Neil Parker, an economist at the Royal Bank of Scotland.  \par \par \uc2 Just as the yuan would be the flavor of choice for investors, bears are taking swipes at the dollar. Massive twin deficits - a current account shortfall and a fast expanding hole in the federal budget - make the dollar vulnerable to collapse, argue the likes of Morgan Stanley economist Stephen Roach.  \par \par \uc2 It has already been sliding, having fallen to five-year lows on a trade-weighted basis this week.  \par \par \uc2 Still, shorting the dollar has its own problems. No country really wants to appreciate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback, so there's the ever-present risk of significant official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been almost as aggressive as China in seeking to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a tight dollar band. And plenty of investors wonder at what point might the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seek to limit the euro's thus-far heady appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Even so, both the yen and the euro are expected to gain ground - though neither \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an unqualified buy.  \par \par \uc2 For instance, Deutsche Bank economists are targeting the yen to gain to 102 against the dollar on a 12-month view, from just under 110 now. That's based on the view that the Japanese economy should do particularly well out of a global recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But the yen also has its detractors. Japanese equities and bonds yield so little that investors don't have much to reward them if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  call doesn't pan out, points out RBS' Parker.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, many economists see the euro advancing to around 1.25 against the dollar from the current 1.18 and parity just a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 Some, like the hedge-fund guru Jim Rogers, think that while the near-term outlook for the euro looks OK, it's still vulnerable: They even question its long-term viability as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the lack of trade imbalances, the E.U. Stability Pact and lackluster local economies mean that the euro, and more specifically euro-denominated bonds, are still a good bet, argues HSBC economist David Bloom. Counter to some who argue that weak economies make the euro a poor proposition, Bloom points to the yen's strength through the series of Japanese recessions during the past dozen years.  \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, because Asian governments are more likely to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market than the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pressure on the dollar should be manifested by relatively bigger depreciation against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Even so, investors who want to reserve judgment on the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have a sample of more peripheral ones to choose from.  \par \par \uc2 Commodity-driven economies are back in favor with an upsurge in raw goods prices. Both the Australian and New Zealand dollars have been flavors of the season, though their advance may have gone too far, suggests RBS' Parker.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the Australian dollar may be a good way to get exposure to China - Australia is a big exporter to China - says HSBC's Bloom.  \par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar is also seen as a potential winner, particularly if the U.S. economy maintains its upward momentum. Canada is one of the biggest exporters to its giant neighbor, and its economy is also significantly driven by demand for commodities.  \par \par \uc2 Back in Europe, Scandinavian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also seen as attractive.  \par \par \uc2 The Swedish krona is positively correlated with pickups in global growth, and the authorities are amenable to modest appreciation, says Trevor Dinmore, an economist at Deutsche Bank. There may also be a pickup with the fading of political concerns raised by the recently failed euro referendum.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc, on the other hand, can't shoulder as much appreciation as it has faced in the past, and the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it won't allow further strength.  \par \par \uc2 And, finally, there's the pound. With Britain also facing trade and budget deficits, its profile looks more like the U.S.'s, though because the shortfalls aren't as big, it should gain on the dollar but weaken against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 (Alen Mattich has been writing Asset Class since its inception in 2002, having been a financial journalist for nearly 15 years, the past seven with Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 44-20-7842-9286 or by e-mail: alen.mattich@dowjones.com)  \par \par \uc2 70779  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031008dza8000mu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
