\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:Dai-Ichi Life May Favor Unhedged Foreign Bonds  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hiroshi Inoue  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 12:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's second-largest life insurer, Dai-Ichi Mutual Life Insurance Co. (DIM.YY), will scale back purchases of foreign bonds over the next six months after hefty buying in the previous half year.  \par \par \uc2 But the life insurer may boost the proportion of foreign bonds it buys unhedged against possible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses if the yen continues to appreciate, according to an investment manager at the firm.  \par \par \uc2 The insurer expects the U.S. and Japan could together act to stop the dollar falling if it heads toward Y100, which could turn around the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and provide an opportunity for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains for Japanese investors placing money in foreign markets.  \par \par \uc2 "If the yen strengthens further, such as toward Y100 (against the dollar), we would consider buying foreign bonds without hedging and selling foreign bonds with hedging," said Masakazu Nakamura, manager of investment planning at Dai-Ichi Life. "We are taking a flexible stance."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's slide to a fresh three-year low Tuesday of Y109.35 from about Y118 at the start of the fiscal year took Dai-Ichi by surprise. Looking ahead, Nakamura expects the dollar-yen rate will be between Y105 and Y120 in the second fiscal half to March 31.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar-yen may temporarily break Y100," he said. "But the U.S. authorities don't want the dollar to sharply weaken as they want to keep stable fund inflows to the U.S. from overseas," he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise won't change the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trend. But coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. would have an impact, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            About 70% of Foreign Assets Hedged \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The decision to slow purchases of foreign bonds overall follows strong buying by Dai-Ichi and other life insurers in recent years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We plan to slightly increase our holdings of foreign bonds in October to March as we already bought a huge amount of foreign bonds between April and September," Nakamura said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The firm bought a net Y700 billion in foreign bonds that were hedged against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk in the first half of the fiscal year starting April 1 and purchased a further Y70 billion to Y80 billion that weren't hedged. The buying was split evenly between dollar-denominated and euro-denominated bonds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the end of March, Dai-Ichi Life's assets totaled Y28.91 trillion. Of the Y3.81 trillion invested in foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denominated assets, 54.5% were in dollars and 36.5% were denominated in euros. While not providing exact figures, Nakamura said those ratios hadn't changed between March and September.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ratio of assets that were hedged against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk rose to more than 70% by the end of September from 60% at the end of March, he added. Nakamura said that the company doesn't plan to increase the ratio.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                 Wants More Credit Exposure \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In domestic markets, Dai-Ichi wants to boost its exposure to corporate loans and credit market securities while reducing its equity holdings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unlike other life insurers, which have cut corporate lending, Dai-Ichi increased loans to companies by a net Y300 billion in the first half, the fund manager said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We would like to increase our holdings of credit risk assets, such as loans and corporate bonds, in the second half of the fiscal year," Nakamura said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the bond market, Dai-Ichi may buy more 10-year government bonds if the yield on the securities rises above 1.5%. The yield closed Wednesday at 1.335% and Nakamura expects it to range between 1.0% and 1.8% in the second half.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have no plans to actively deal JGBs given the current levels, but if the 10-year bond yield rises above 1.5%, we would consider buying bonds," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, "depending on interest rate levels, we could lengthen duration of our JGB holdings by selling 10-year bonds and buying 20- and 30-year bonds as we did between April and September," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dai-Ichi Life's holdings of domestic bonds fell by Y200 billion in the first half. "We sold a net Y300 billion in government bonds and bought about Y100 billion in corporate bonds and securitized products," Nakamura said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the equity market, the firm sold a net Y100 billion in domestic shares in the first half and will further reduce its holdings in the second half, he said. The Nikkei 225 Stock Index will move in a range between 9500 and 12000 in the second half, Nakamura predicted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The firm expects Japan's economic recovery, which was stronger than expected in the first half, will moderate in the next six months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dai-Ichi Life, along with Japan's six other major life insurers, will release earnings results for the first half on Nov. 27.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-9203; hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031008dza8000mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
