\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 8  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,171 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 11:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Standard & Poor's Ratings Services raised its sovereign ratings for Malaysia, Thailand, and Indonesia on Wednesday, commending the countries on improved fiscal conditions, stronger external liquidity, and growing economic stability. (WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed substantially Wednesday to new long-term highs against the dollar as the market pushes more of the burden of external imbalances in the U.S. economy onto this region's economies - whether governments here like it or not. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Like It Or Not, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Looks More Flexible" ran at 0720 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan will continue to act in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if exchange rates move in "unnatural" ways, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Wednesday, after the dollar plunged to its lowest level since November 2000 overnight. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tanigaki-2: \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Like Playing A Game Vs Mkt" ran at 0114 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged for the 16th consecutive month, but economists Wednesday agreed that the case for hikes will soon become compelling as growth rebounds. ("=WRAP: Australian Rates Steady But Hikes Now Within View" ran at 0113 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The capital investment recovery that has powered Japan's recent economic expansion looked shakier Wednesday, after data showed machinery orders by Japanese companies were surprisingly weak in August, raising concerns that the surge in spending on new plant and equipment may cool in coming months. ("=WRAP: Japan Machine Orders Slide, Clouding Capex Outlook" ran at 0811 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Investors smoothly absorbed Sumitimo Mitsui Banking Corp.'s sizable Y100 billion five-year bond offering Wednesday, proof that the appetite for corporate bonds at the start of the second fiscal half remains robust. (WRAP will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: PT Indonesian Satellite Corp., or Indosat, has raised the size of its planned local bond offering to IDR2.5 trillion from IDR1.75 trillion due to good demand, sources familiar with the deal said Wednesday. Indonesia's second-largest telephone company has also set the coupon for the five-year tranche of the issue at 12.5% and the coupon for the seven-year tranche at 12.875%, the sources told Dow Jones. ("Indonesian Satellite Sets Bond Coupons, Raises Issue Size" ran at 0151 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Foreign direct investment in South Korea continued to decline in the third quarter, largely due to high domestic wages and increasing competition from China. The investment slump is damping the nation's hopes of becoming a regional business hub, economists said. ("=WRAP: S Korea 3Q FDI Declines On Competition From China" ran at 0916 GMT; see also "S Korea's Exports Of IT Goods +26% On Yr To $5.03B In Sep" that ran at 0236 GMT and "S Korea/Aug Service Output" that ran at 0342 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The main target of last month's G-7 statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was China, not Japan as some believe, a former top U.S. Treasury Department official said. But it's not clear if Washington, which is moving into election mode ahead of a presidential poll next year, is really serious about pushing China to ditch its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, or is just trying to impress voters, said Kenneth Dam, an ex-Deputy Treasury Secretary. ("=INTERVIEW: G7 Target Was China, Not Japan - Ex-Tsy Dam" ran at 0810 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's second-largest life insurer, Dai-Ichi Mutual Life Insurance, will scale back buying of foreign bonds over the next six months after big purchases in the previous half year. But the insurer may increase the proportion of foreign bonds it buys unhedged for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk if the yen continues to appreciate, according to an investment manager at the firm. (INTERVIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Fukoku Mutual Life Insurance, a medium-sized Japanese life insurer, expects the dollar to further weaken and if so, would like to buy foreign bonds without \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging in the second fiscal half. (INTERVIEW will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's government Wednesday detailed long-awaited legislation designed to raise the bar on auditor independence and corporate disclosure, while boosting shareholder rights. The rules will be introduced into parliament in December and are predominantly a result of the A$5.3 billion implosion of HIH Insurance in 2001, Australia's biggest corporate failure, and the collapse of telecom group One.Tel Ltd. ("=WRAP: Australia Drafts Bill To Tighten Corporate Rules" ran at 0722 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Cosco Shipping said Wednesday its supervisory board has approved a proposal to issue convertible bonds of up to 1.03 billion yuan. ("China Cosco Supervisory Bd Approves Convertible Bond Plan" ran at 0116 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's economy likely grew by 4.14% on year in the July-September period, higher than the 3.76% growth in the second quarter, the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday. Official third-quarter GDP figures will be released by the central statistics bureau later this month. ("Bk Indonesia/3Q GDP" ran at 0711 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's economy likely grew an average 1.3% on year in the third quarter, after a 4.2% contraction in April-June, according to a Dow Jones survey of economists. he range of forecasts is for growth of between 0.5% and 2%. GDP data will be released 0000 GMT Friday. ("=PREVIEW: Singapore 3Q GDP Likely Resumed Growing Broadly" ran at 0730 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Strong New Zealand retail sales data Wednesday suggest the domestic economy still has plenty of momentum, which could trigger higher interest rates in the first half of next year, particularly if the world economic recovery alleviates the country's current weak spot - exports. ("=DATA VIEW: What's That Humming Sound Coming From NZ?" ran at 0107 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The housing market in New Zealand's biggest city, Auckland, appears to have paid no heed to a warning from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was intended to damp rising house prices. ("NZ Auckland House Market Shrugs Off \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Warning" ran at 1700 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar drifted lower against the yen in Asia Wednesday, but remained off its overnight three-year low as market participants warily pondered where the pair's near-term bottom might lie. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0535 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's No. 3 mobile operator Far EasTone Telecommunications finally signed off on its merger with smaller rival KG Telecommunications, in a key deal that may well reshape the island's overcrowded telecommunications landscape. The NT$30 billion deal - Taiwan's largest telecom industry merger to date - will enable Far EasTone to tie up with Japan's NTT DoCoMo, and help Far Eastone steal a march on local rivals in the race to third-generation rollout down the road. ("=WRAP: Far EasTone Tie-Up With DoCoMo Via KG Tel Merger" will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group on Wednesday raised its earnings outlook for the fiscal half ended Sept. 30, citing stock-valuation gains and returned tax payments from the Tokyo government. The banking group also said it will set up an investment fund with Goldman Sachs Group and others to accelerate its bad loan cleanup. ("Sumitomo Mitsui" first take ran at 0723 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hanaro Telecom may have signed a $1.1 billion financing package with American International Group and Newbridge Capital last month, but a renewed proxy fight and a potential court battle cast further doubt on the high-speed Internet access provider's financing prospects.("=Proxy Fight Intensifies Duel For S Korea Hanaro Telecom" ran at 0832 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: State-owned Krung Thai Bank said Wednesday it has priced its public offering at 8.50 baht a share to raise up to THB29.33 billion, in what will be Thailand's largest share sale since the flotation of state-owned PTT in November 2001. ("=WRAP: Krung Thai Bank Sets Offer Price At THB8.50/Shr" ran at 0629 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: The Thai unit of ABN Amro Holding, ABN Amro Asia Securities, will merge with local brokerage firm Asset Plus Securities to create the country's largest securities firm with market capitalization of 12 billion baht. (WRAP will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's Qantas Airways and its trans-Tasman rival Air New Zealand may have to wait at least six months before an appeal into their proposed alliance begins. The Australian Competition Tribunal said Wednesday that while it wants to expedite the appeal process, it probably won't start the hearing until April 2004, to give the parties involved time to collect evidence and prepare. ("=WRAP: Australia's Qantas, Air NZ Appeal Still Mos Away" ran at 0655 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Japanese automakers' share prices drove off a cliff recently due to a rapidly strengthening yen, but that's put them at attractive levels considering their decent business outlook, market players say. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Japan Automakers Oversold On Yen Rise" ran at 0635 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Healthy television ratings, a resurgence in advertising and one-off tax benefits led Australia's Ten Network Holdings to post an A$89 million net profit for fiscal 2003. ("=WRAP:Australia's Ten Posts A$89M Profit, Ad Market Booms" ran at 0830 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: With A$110 million wiped off its market value, Australian biotechnology company Peptech said Wednesday it will consider legal action if it can't resolve a licensing dispute with Abbott Laboratories Inc. ("=INTERVIEW: Peptech Talks To Lawyers About Abbott Dispute"  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Prudential Financial is in the last stage of talks to take over management control of South Korea's CJ Investment Securities and is likely to reach an agreement as early as this month, said an official at CJ Investment Wednesday. ("Prudential Fincl Near Closing Talks To Control CJ Invest" ran at 0056 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Cheung Kong Infrastructure Holdings said Wednesday it is considering a bid for A$2.6 billion worth of gas pipeline assets owned by Australia's Epic Energy. ("HK Cheung Kong Infra May Bid For Epic Energy Assets" ran at 0336 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: U.S. energy company TXU Corp. said Wednesday it appointed advisers to examine the merits of a partial share market listing of its Australian subsidiary by next year. ("TXU Australia/Advisers" ran at 0034 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Taiyo Life Insurance, Daido Life Insurance and T&D Financial Life Insurance, which will integrate \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the umbrella of a newly created holding company, aim to slash costs by Y21 billion or more over the next five years. (story will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Fujitsu said Wednesday it is in talks with several microchip suppliers in an attempt to expand its chip-making foundry \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Fujitsu In Talks With Other Co To Expand Chip Foundry Ops" ran at 0226 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: FamilyMart aims to open 200 convenience stores in the U.S. by the fiscal year ending February 2007 that will feature Japanese-style food products such as sushi boxed lunches, a FamilyMart spokesman said Wednesday. ("FamilyMart To Open 200 Convenience Stores In US By 2007" ran at 0514 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: PSA Corp., the world's second- busiest port operator, is keen to buy into ports in India and South Africa, resuming an overseas expansion drive that's been overshadowed by domestic restructuring in the past year, an official at the Singapore-government owned company said Wednesday. (INTERVIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: U.S. hotel organization Best Western International is pursuing frequent flyer cooperation deals with China's major domestic airlines as a means of promoting its China market expansion, a Best Western executive said Wednesday. (INTERVIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Resources Power, which is aiming to list on Hong Kong's main board early next month, will buy a 10% stake in China's largest independent power producer, Huaneng International Power Development Corp., before the listing, a person familiar with the deal said Wednesday. ("CR Power To Buy 10% Of China Huaneng Before Float -Source" first ran at 0209 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Indian software firms expect strong second- quarter results on the back of increased sales, with industry major Infosys Technologies likely raising again its earnings guidance for the full year. Software companies will start announcing their quarterly results this week. ("=India IT Cos Expect Strong 2Q; Higher Infosys Guidance" ran at 0514 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Liquidity on the Singapore Exchange's Middle East crude oil futures contract is steadily drying up and many traders feel it's only a matter of time before SGX gives up on its sickly infant. ("=Clock Ticks For SGX Oil Futures, Traders Spurn Contract" ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031008dza8000hk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
