\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Bank Of China Just What Tsy Market Needs  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Agnes T. Crane  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 18:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its quicksilver \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market may cause hearts to beat a little faster in the bond market, but the People's Bank of China, although staid and stuffy by comparison, promises to be a steady buyer of Treasurys over the long haul.  \par \par \uc2 As the market faces a steady beat of reasons to sell government securities - a rebounding U.S. economy, a ballooning federal budget deficit, a falling dollar and early signs of an improving labor market - a reliable buyer of government securities becomes more attractive to dealers hoping to find a home for an ever-increasing supply of issuance.  \par \par \uc2 Whereas the billions of dollars pouring into Treasurys from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which reinvests the bulk of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proceeds in government securities - could slow or cease entirely if the dollar stabilizes or rises moderately, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep on buying no matter where the dollar goes.  \par \par \uc2 The difference between the Treasury market suitors lies in the reasons they have so many dollars to spend. The Bank of Japan is trying to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much against the dollar, something that would hurt its export sector and undermine its fight against deflation.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of China is running an enormous trade surplus with the U.S., one that due to rigid capital controls leaves it flush with dollars that need to be reinvested. This trend is unlikely to change and will intensify if the surplus continues to grow, even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bows to international pressure and weakens the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as we have this out-of-whack trade balance...they are going to continue to be a pretty good buyer" of U.S. debt, including Treasurys and agencies, said Craig Coats, head of fixed income at Keefe, Bruyette & Woods in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "If there is some kind of revaluation, I don't think this is going to make a dent in trade balance revenues," Coats said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    The Peg, Nonconvertibility Certainly Helps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since 1998, the Bank of China has married the fortunes of the yuan and the dollar in a managed float, in which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair was permitted to fluctuate by 0.5% from the anchor of CNY8.277.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the value of the dollar plummeting, the peg becomes problematic for China's trading partners since, they argue, it gives that nation's exporters an unfair advantage. In fact, China's trade surplus with the U.S. has swelled to $65.3 billion in the first seven months of this year from $52.5 billion from the year before.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is nonconvertible and exporters must exchange their dollar proceeds for yuan with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Bank of China is left holding more and more dollars in its reserves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Combine that with a surge in foreign direct investment, and China has a lot of dollars to put to work.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Anybody that can legally get yuan wants to," said Greg Jensen, senior research associate at institutional money manager Bridgewater Associates in Westport, Conn., who believes the hunger by foreigners for anything yuan turns up the pressure on the peg.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saber-rattling from politicians in U.S. and the call by Group of Seven industrial nations for more flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regimes has fed speculation that an appreciation of the yuan is on the horizon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While Jensen expects this hammering at the peg to force the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's hand sometime over the next few months, other analysts believe a change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime is anything but near as China's financial system makes revaluation dangerous for the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We don't feel that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  revaluation is desirable or likely," said Amy Falls, global bond strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York. "We're not factoring in a major change in Chinese appetite for U.S. bonds," she said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Shift From Agencies To Treasurys \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the U.S. federal budget turned to surplus in the late 1990s and Treasury issuance actually dwindled, the Bank of China along with many investors began to view agency debt - securities issued by mortgage giants Fannie Mae (FNM) and Freddie Mac (FRE) - as a substitute for the traditional safe haven of government securities and targeted their dollars on this growing market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts believe China earmarked the bulk of its dollars for agency investment during this period. With the U.S. federal budget back in the red, China has begun to reconsider its allocations between Treasurys and agencies, said Jensen who believes the current mix of China's holdings is approximately 50:50.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In July, China's net purchases of Treasurys totaled $3.7 billion and of agencies $3.6 billion, according to data from the U.S. Treasury.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The changed dynamics in the U.S. debt markets, including more Treasury supply and the political firestorm surrounding government-sponsored enterprises is likely to tilt the balance toward Treasurys, said Jensen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031008dza80006w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
