\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar eases vs euro, yen tamed by BOJ shadow.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2003\par 08:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 7 (Reuters) - The dollar eased against the euro on Tuesday after its bounce inspired by last week's strong U.S. jobs data proved short-lived and reinforced the market's bearish sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady against the yen, though mired within one percent of last week's three-year lows around 110.10 yen amid wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is not so simple as to believe that things have turned around just because payroll figures were good for one month," said Satoru Ogasawara, a strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston.  \par \par \uc2 He said Washington's current account deficit and budget deficit, exacerbated by ballooning Iraq-related costs, would likely weigh on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some time to come.  \par \par \uc2 With concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shackling dollar/yen, the main beneficiary of the dollar's weakness was the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "From a fundamentals standpoint, the economic recovery in the euro zone is clearly lagging that in the United States and Japan, so the euro's gains are just the flip side of the dollar's weakness," Ogasawara said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at $1.1715/21 at 0547 GMT, up slightly from around $1.1705 late in New York on Monday and within sight of three-month highs around $1.1770 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed losses it had made in the wake of Friday's U.S. jobs data and bounced by one percent on European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's comments in a newspaper article that the dollar's decline was "unavoidable".  \par \par \uc2 RISING RESERVES  \par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar hovered around 111 yen, virtually unchanged on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said interest among Japanese exporters to sell the dollar on any significant rally was keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below 112 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though yen bulls are squaring up their long yen positions now, exporters and real-money people have fallen behind in their yen-buying and are now waiting for their chance to do so," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust & Custody Services Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "There are plenty of people who would be delighted at the opportunity to sell dollars at levels above 112 yen."  \par \par \uc2 Yet, a string of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, including one conducted through the U.S. Federal Reserve, was seen easing pressure on the dollar to test 110 yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data early on Tuesday showed that Japan's foreign reserves rose to $604.873 billion at the end of September from $555.088 billion at the end of August - a result of the ministry's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the month.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry said last week that it had conducted 4.46 trillion yen ($40.18 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the month to September 26, bringing the year-to-date total to around 13.5 trillion yen - already a record for a calendar year.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market's focus was now on U.S. corporate earnings results for the third quarter, which start coming out in earnest later on Tuesday. "We're expecting fairly good figures," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "But such figures may only add to the bullishness in Japanese shares instead of helping the dollar. The correlation between U.S. stock prices and the dollar has not been very high lately."  \par \par \uc2 Big firms such as aluminium producer Alcoa Inc, a Dow Industrials component, and Pepsico Inc were due to release their earnings on Tuesday. ($1=110.98 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031007dza7000u0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
