\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Ends Mostly Lower, Hits Three-Year Low vs. Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2003\par 23:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar plunged to fresh three-year lows against the yen Tuesday and only managed to claw back some of these losses on what traders suspected was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities.  \par \par \uc2 In the absence of hard economic data, dealers resumed the heavy dollar selling of recent weeks and pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower across the board.  \par \par \uc2 Apart from the dollar's slide against the yen, which broke the psychological support threshold of 110 yen, the dollar sank to seven-year lows against the Canadian dollar, six-year lows against the Australian dollar, and three-and-a-half-year lows against the South African rand.  \par \par \uc2 The exception to this trend Tuesday was sterling, which fell across the board after the release of weaker-than-expected United Kingdom August industrial-production and manufacturing data.  \par \par \uc2 By far the biggest story of the session was the yen and the suspected Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "It's surprising, I was expecting [dollar] consolidation above 110 yen before eventually going back down again ... but we're in a very dangerous zone right now," said a senior dealer at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had slumped earlier Tuesday to as low as 109.35 yen - a level not seen since November 2000 - before beginning a slow recovery when it suddenly jumped more than half a yen back above 110 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said Japanese banks in New York and Tokyo were behind the dollar's sharp, but brief move up, which they said was covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acting on behalf of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday in New York, the dollar was at 109.90 yen, around a full yen, or 1%, weaker than 110.93 yen late Monday in New York. The euro was at 129.32 yen, down from 129.96 yen late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fared better against the dollar, nearing the close of New York trading at $1.1778, up from $1.1718 late Monday. It had climbed to a near four month-high of $1.1813 earlier Tuesday before selling by some smaller European central banks above $1.18 and selling against the yen took it lower again, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar slipped to 1.3146 francs, from 1.3197 francs late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's concern over the dollar centers on the yawning U.S. trade and current account deficits. Analysts say the latter is running at around $550 billion a year, or about 5% of the country's gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven communique on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  published on Sept. 20, which called for more "flexibility" in exchange rates, has been interpreted by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers as tacit approval for a weaker dollar, which will help redress the U.S. current account imbalance.  \par \par \uc2 And it is Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are expected to take on the lion's share of this readjustment, which, added to global investors' appetite for Japanese assets, is partly why the yen is appreciating so fast.  \par \par \uc2 What is more, it looks like the dollar will only continue its slide against the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York, said that the effectiveness of the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday is open to question.  \par \par \uc2 "If it is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's not causing the dollar bears much pain," he said, adding that that would require a push back up through 110.50 yen. "As long as we stay below 110.50 yen, that means it's probably headed lower, so it's better to sell into rallies."  \par \par \uc2 The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a Japanese bank in London agreed.  \par \par \uc2 "We've gone through 110.00 yen without a meltdown, but a close in New York [Tuesday] below 110.00 yen and it could get nasty," he said.  \par \par \uc2 And the Japanese bank dealer in New York said Tokyo dealers will get a shock when they wake up to see the dollar below 110.00 yen, a level which many had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend in order to keep Japanese exports competitive on world markets.  \par \par \uc2 "Exporters still have a bunch of dollars to sell," he said, adding however, that the lower the dollar goes, the more buying interest from Japanese importers will emerge.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the dollar slid to a seven-year low of C$1.3270 before recovering a bit to close at C$1.3324, and a 30-month low against the South African rand of 6.7850 rand before closing local trading at around 6.8376 rand.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar also capitalized on the greenback's woes Tuesday, surging through 69 U.S. cents to hit a six-year high of 69.19 U.S. cents.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of Australia sets interest rates Wednesday and is expected to leave the benchmark cash rate unchanged at 4.75%.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling gave up ground to the dollar, falling nearly a cent to $1.6618 from $1.6702 late Monday, after a decline in manufacturing production in August took markets by surprise. Manufacturing output fell 0.6% on the month, compared with expectations for a rise of 0.2%, and after rises of 0.3% in July and June.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of England holds its monetary-policy meeting Wednesday and Thursday and is expected to leave rates unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Tom Barkley in New York contributed to this report.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031007dza70003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
