\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 7  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2003\par 12:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (In the item timed around 0907 GMT, the timing for the JPMorgan Chase Bank story was incomplete.)  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit their highest level ever of $604.87 billion in September, jumping a record $49.79 billion from a month earlier due to massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Foreign Reserves" first take ran at 2356 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: History isn't on the Japanese government's side in its battle to keep the yen from strengthening in the face of an improving economy. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: History Not On Tokyo's Side in Dlr/Yen Battle" ran at 0619 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: As the global economy recovers, Singapore's gross domestic product could expand by as much as 5% in 2004, much faster than the at best 1% growth expected this year, Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong said Tuesday, in the government's most optimistic economic prognosis so far for the year ahead. ("=WRAP: Singapore GDP May Grow As Much As 5% In '04 - Lee" ran at 0700 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: State-owned Singapore Power is planning to offer US$1.6 billion and S$1 billion portions out of its proposed US$2 billion dual-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  global bond, sources familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Tuesday. And as Singapore Power's plan to issue US$2 billion worth of global bonds gets underway, Standard & Poor's warned that the state-owned utility's long-term ratings could come under risk from taking on higher debt. ("=Singapore Power/Bond" first take ran at 0838 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Though the Hong Kong dollar has now spent more than two weeks trading above the rate at which it is pegged to the U.S. dollar, the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems in no hurry to force the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down to the formal rate of 7.8. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: HKMA Seems Happy With Stronger Hong Kong Dollar" ran at 0353 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: As dollar-yen trading has come to look like a battleground between the G-7 and Bank of Japan, the euro has been gathering attention, with its spot-market climb sending options prices spiking. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views:EUR/USD Vol Pricey But Still Room To Run" ran at 0422 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The Taiwan government said Tuesday exports rose 11.6% on year in September, while imports grew 4.1%. Exports totaled US$12.56 billion last month and imports amounted to US$10.98 billion, giving a surplus of US$1.58 billion. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan Exports Rise On Improving Global Demand" first take ran at 0839 GMT; Data View will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The value of Hong Kong's retail sales grew a better-than-expected 1.2% by value in August from a year earlier, ending a long-running declining trend. The growth was due to a recovery in local demand after the SARS outbreak and a strong rebound in tourism led by China visitors, the government said Tuesday. ("DATA SNAP: HK August Retail Sales Halt On-Year Fall" first take ran at 0842 GMT; Data View will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's finance minister Tanigaki Tuesday stood firm on Tokyo's commitment to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to smooth excessive fluctuations, a practice which he says has the backing of the international community. ("Japan Tanigaki" first take ran at 0123 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese Banking and Economy Minister Takenaka says he wants to consider increasing tax breaks for bad loan disposals. Despite requests in previous years by regulators, the Finance Ministry has rebuffed calls for such tax breaks because of the massive costs in terms of tax revenue. ("Japan Takenaka" first take ran at 0045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ Watch: While some economists expect the Bank of Japan to address recent yen strength by boosting liquidity, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board will likely keep policy steady when it meets this week due to clear signs Japan's economy remains on a recovery track. The two-day meeting starts Thursday. ("=BOJ Watch: Policy Likely Steady Friday Despite Yen Gains" ran at 0614 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Greater competition and pressure to expand abroad are among the reasons behind the deteriorating credit quality of government-linked companies in Singapore, once considered the safest borrowers in the country, Standard & Poor's said Tuesday. ("=WRAP: Singapore Govt-Linked Cos' Credit Quality To Fall" first take ran at 0810 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: The New Zealand dollar continues to find favor as a niche investment \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  internationally, but the driving force behind offshore Kiwi dollar bond issuance is now coming from Japanese retail investors rather than the euromarkets and European investors. ("=ASIA DEBT: Recent NZD Uridashi Issuance Exceeds Eurokiwi" ran at 0125 GMT)  \par \par 