w device is strategically important for Sony as it aims to catch up with its domestic peers in the rapidly expanding DVD/HDD recorder market. ("Sony To Launch Game/DVD Recorder Device In Japan Year-End" ran at 0422 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese pharmaceutical company Sankyo said Tuesday its U.S. business partner Cygnus Inc. has filed a lawsuit against it seeking US$450 million in damages for an alleged breach of contract. ("Sankyo -2: Sankyo's U.S. Unit Sells Cygnus Equipment" first take ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's MediaTek, the world's largest designer of chips for DVD and CD disc drives, reported monthly revenue in September rose by nearly 50% on year as the company enjoyed strength in all product segments. Analysts believe the company's October revenue will be even better. ("Taiwan's Mediatek" ran at 0101 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Shinhan Card, a unit of Shinhan Financial Group, will issue EUR300 million of five-year asset-backed securities Tuesday, said the company in a disclosure to the Financial Supervisory Service. ("S Korea Shinhan Card/ABS" ran at 0139 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: With Indonesian interest rates set to bottom out and the stock market up more than 40% so far this year and expected to head even higher, more of the country's companies are likely to go public in 2004, the president of the Jakarta Stock Exchange said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW: Jakarta Bourse Pres Expects More IPOs In 2004" ran at 0754 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: With plans for a A$10 million listing before Christmas, the timing shown by executives at Nickel Australia couldn't be better. Nickel Australia, a spin-off from gold producer Croesus Mining, believes that it will be well received by investors, despite worries from some analysts that nickel share prices are overheating. ("=INTERVIEW: Nickel Australia's Timing Couldn't Be Better" ran at 0543 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: In a sign that Asia-Pacific mergers and acquisitions may pick up, JPMorgan Chase Bank plans to hire as many as 150 more staff across the region annually from next year, a top regional executive said. (story will run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Despite the collapse of merger talks with two banking group suitors recently, analysts still recommend shares of financial services group AMMB Holdings as a buy. Malaysia's fifth-largest banking group is likely to remain among the country's top banks even without a merger, they say, citing attractive valuation, improved profit outlook, as well as better asset quality and loans growth in the medium term. ("=Asian Stock Focus:Buy Malaysia AMMB Despite Failed Deal" ran at 0608 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Technologies, the majority government-owned defense and engineering conglomerate, will report fiscal third-quarter earnings later today. (results will run around 0900 GMT; WRAP on merit)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Yue Yuen, the world's largest athletic and casual footwear maker, will release third-quarter results later today. Net profit likely rose more than 30% on year helped by strong back-to-school orders and stabilizing price trends. (results will run around 0900 GMT or later)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031007dza7000fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
