apan's finance minister Tanigaki Tuesday stood firm on Tokyo's commitment to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to smooth excessive fluctuations, a practice which he says has the backing of the international community. ("Japan Tanigaki" first take ran at 0123 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese Banking and Economy Minister Takenaka says he wants to consider increasing tax breaks for bad loan disposals. Despite requests in previous years by regulators, the Finance Ministry has rebuffed calls for such tax breaks because of the massive costs in terms of tax revenue. ("Japan Takenaka" first take ran at 0045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ Watch: While some economists expect the Bank of Japan to address recent yen strength by boosting liquidity, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board will likely keep policy steady when it meets this week due to clear signs Japan's economy remains on a recovery track. The two-day meeting starts Thursday. ("=BOJ Watch: Policy Likely Steady Friday Despite Yen Gains" ran at 0614 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Greater competition and pressure to expand abroad are among the reasons behind the deteriorating credit quality of government-linked companies in Singapore, once considered the safest borrowers in the country, Standard & Poor's said Tuesday. ("=WRAP: Singapore Govt-Linked Cos' Credit Quality To Fall" first take ran at 0810 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: The New Zealand dollar continues to find favor as a niche investment \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  internationally, but the driving force behind offshore Kiwi dollar bond issuance is now coming from Japanese retail investors rather than the euromarkets and European investors. ("=ASIA DEBT: Recent NZD Uridashi Issuance Exceeds Eurokiwi" ran at 0125 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand economy likely rebounded in the third quarter to grow faster than forecast in the government's May budget, according to the Treasury Department. ("NZ Treasury Says 3Q Growth May Beat Budget Forecast" ran at 2013 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Direct investment in Vietnam was up 7.4% on year to 149.8 trillion dong in the first nine months of 2003, the Government Statistics Office said in a report Tuesday. ("Vietnam 9-Mo Direct Invest +7.4%; Govt Accounts For Half" ran at 0710 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar edged slightly higher against the yen in Asia Tuesday as buying by Japanese banks on behalf of local companies overcame a bout of sales by Japanese exporters. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0541 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sumitomo Life Realty (N.Y.) Inc., a real-estate arm of Sumitomo Life Insurance Co., and Hines of the U.S. have jointly established a Y64 billion (US$580 million) real-estate investment trust, or REIT, in the U.S. Sumitomo Life Insurance, the third-largest life insurer in Japan, plans to increase the REIT to Y165 billion ($1.5 billion) over the next few years. Sumitomo Life Insurance is the first Japanese financial institution to establish a REIT in the U.S. ("Japan's Sumitomo Life, US Hines Set Up Y64B REIT In US" ran at 0140 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's Computershare is eyeing Georgeson Shareholder Communications Inc., about seven months after the New York-based proxy solicitation firm put itself up for sale. ("=WRAP: Australia's Computershare Targets US Georgeson" ran at 0646 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sony said Tuesday it will begin domestic sales of a home-use device that can function as both a "PlayStation 2" game console and a DVD/hard-disc drive recorder at the end of the year. The new device is strategically important for Sony as it aims to catch up with its domestic peers in the rapidly expanding DVD/HDD recorder market. ("Sony To Launch Game/DVD Recorder Device In Japan Year-End" ran at 0422 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese pharmaceutical company Sankyo said Tuesday its U.S. business partner Cygnus Inc. has filed a lawsuit against it seeking US$450 million in damages for an alleged breach of contract. ("Sankyo -2: Sankyo's U.S. Unit Sells Cygnus Equipment" first take ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's MediaTek, the world's largest designer of chips for DVD and CD disc drives, reported monthly revenue in September rose by nearly 50% on year as the company enjoyed strength in all product segments. Analysts believe the company's October revenue will be even better. ("Taiwan's Mediatek" ran at 0101 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Shinhan Card, a unit of Shinhan Financial Group, will issue EUR300 million of five-year asset-backed securities Tuesday, said the company in a disclosure to the Financial Supervisory Service. ("S Korea Shinhan Card/ABS" ran at 0139 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: With Indonesian interest rates set to bottom out and the stock market up more than 40% so far this year and expected to head even higher, more of the country's companies are likely to go public in 2004, the president of the Jakarta Stock Exchange said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW: Jakarta Bourse Pres Expects More IPOs In 2004" ran at 0754 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: With plans for a A$10 million listing before Christmas, the timing shown by executives at Nickel Australia couldn't be better. Nickel Australia, a spin-off from gold producer Croesus Mining, believes that it will be well received by investors, despite worries from some analysts that nickel share prices are overheating. ("=INTERVIEW: Nickel Australia's Timing Couldn't Be Better" ran at 0543 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: In a sign that Asia-Pacific mergers and acquisitions may pick up, JPMorgan Chase Bank plans to hire as many as 150 more staff across the region annually from next year, a top regional executive said. (story will run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Despite the collapse of merger talks with two banking group suitors recently, analysts still recommend shares of financial services group AMMB Holdings as a buy. Malaysia's fifth-largest banking group is likely to remain among the country's top banks even without a merger, they say, citing attractive valuation, improved profit outlook, as well as better asset quality and loans growth in the medium term. ("=Asian Stock Focus:Buy Malaysia AMMB Despite Failed Deal" ran at 0608 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Technologies, the majority government-owned defense and engineering conglomerate, will report fiscal third-quarter earnings later today. (results will run around 0900 GMT; WRAP on merit)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Yue Yuen, the world's largest athletic and casual footwear maker, will release third-quarter results later today. Net profit likely rose more than 30% on year helped by strong back-to-school orders and stabilizing price trends. (results will run around 0900 GMT or later)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Hot Tomato, a radio broadcaster that plans to bid for an FM license in Adelaide, could also compete for licenses in other Australian state capitals. The company, backed by Rupert Murdoch's former wife, Anna Mann, and her two brothers Hans and Jaan Torv, is keen to expand beyond its one and only station on Queensland state's Gold Coast. ("=INTERVIEW: Radio Calls Murdoch's Former Wife And Family" ran at 0817 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand fastfood operator Restaurant Brands Tuesday reported a 15% drop in first-half net profit to NZ$4.3 million, hurt by dwindling sales and lower profit margins at KFC. ("NZ's Restaurant Brands/Earnings" ran at 2045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean steel giant Posco's third-quarter net profit likely surged 34% on year to 507.1 billion won, boosted by a series of price hikes since last year, analysts say. Earnings will be released Thursday. ("=S Korea Posco 3Q Net Likely Surged On Steel Price Hikes" ran at 0403 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Cosmo Oil said Tuesday it now expects a group net profit of Y1.9 billion for the April-September period, down from the Y2 billion it projected in late May. The oil refiner attributed the downward revision to rising costs stemming from the closing of some of its service stations. ("Cosmo Oil -2: Costs Of Closing Service Stations" ran at 0627 GMT; see also "=INTERVIEW: Cosmo Oil Cuts Retail Sector, Eyes Gas, Power" that ran at 0715 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Tokyo Electric Power Tuesday kept unchanged its parent pretax profit outlook for this fiscal year despite a nuclear plant safety cover-up scandal, noting that cost- cutting efforts will help cancel out rising fuel costs due to the prolonged shutdown of its nuclear power plants. ("Tepco Keeps Profit Targets Unchanged Despite Fuel Costs" ran at 0650 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesian government next week will offer 10 oil and gas blocks for bidding to investors in a move to boost the country's dwindling oil output, a government official said Tuesday.("Indonesia/Oil Blocks" ran at 0730 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: State-owned Vietnam Oil & Gas Corp., or PetroVietnam, is seeking bank loans of $800 million to help fund two projects in the country's southwest - the Dung Quat oil refinery and the Ca Mau gas-powered fertilizer complex, a company official said Tuesday. ("PetroVietnam Seeks $800M Loan For Dung Quat, Ca Mau Projs" ran at 0357 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031007dza7000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
