\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Edges Up Vs Yen On Japan Corporate Demand  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Harui  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2003\par 07:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar edged slightly higher against the yen in Asia Tuesday as buying by Japanese banks on behalf of local companies overcame a bout of sales by Japanese exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger-than-expected dollar demand among Japanese firms toward the 0100 GMT Tokyo fixing pushed up the dollar to a high of Y111.13. But exporters soon sold the U.S. unit to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, dragging the greenback below the psychologically important level of Y111.  \par \par \uc2 Due to ongoing expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a sharp yen rise, the dollar found solid support in the high-Y110s, where the MOF was suspected to have placed covert dollar bids.  \par \par \uc2 Underscoring Tokyo's willingness to combat yen strength, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters Tuesday, "When the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign (exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market shows irregular movements, we will act appropriately at the appropriate time."  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting the Japanese authorities' massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a record high of $604.87 billion in September, rising a record $49.79 billion from August, according to Finance Ministry data released Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also sought to defuse veiled criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its trading partners, particularly the U.S. Our stance "is an internationally understood (policy)," he said.  \par \par \uc2 In the run-up to U.S. Presidential elections next year, Washington is becoming increasingly worried that U.S. exports could be hurt by other nations using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak against the dollar, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Last Friday, U.S. President George W. Bush said that he is putting pressure on other countries to make sure their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates don't work to undercut U.S. manufacturers.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, it's an election campaign performance (by the Bush Administration) to make U.S. exporters happy," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities SMBC.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y111.05, up from Y110.93 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at Y110.97 late Monday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1712, down slightly from $1.1718 late Monday in New York. The euro was at $1.1577 in Tokyo Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y130.04, up from Y129.96 in New York late Monday. The euro was at Y128.47 in Tokyo late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6748, up from $1.6702 in New York and higher than $1.6612 in Tokyo Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Given a dearth of fresh market-moving incentives this week, the dollar could consolidate in a narrow Y110.20-Y112.20 range until next week, said Minoru Shioiri, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 But, "the dollar is still headed toward more weakness," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Despite better-than-expected U.S. non-farm payrolls data last Friday that spurred dollar short-covering, Shioiri said factors such as the U.S.' massive current-account deficit will likely continue to weigh on the dollar in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 Next week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market could be in for a bumpy ride due to several events, such as the U.S. Treasury's publication of its semiannual \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report on Oct. 15 and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's testimony on the report before the Senate Banking Committee on Oct. 16.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury's report and Snow's testimony are turning into major focal points for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, mainly owing to Snow's recent calls on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a minimum and on China to widen its yuan trading band and ease capital controls.  \par \par \uc2 Bush's scheduled two-day visit to Tokyo next week is also expected to garner attention from market players, especially after Bush's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related comments last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Public broadcaster NHK reported that Bush would be in Tokyo Oct. 17-18. Foreign Ministry and U.S. Embassy officials have refused to comment, citing security concerns.  \par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2