\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls, traders don't see ECB curbing euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier David  \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2003\par 18:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 6 (Reuters) - The dollar fell sharply against major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin trade on Monday, after outgoing European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to sell euros in order to halt its rise.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied after a report on the U.S. labor market showed the economy had added jobs unexpectedly in September. But because the economy has shed nearly 3 million jobs in the past two years, traders entered the U.S. session inclined to sell the dollar anew after having rethought the previous session's bullishness.  \par \par \uc2 With renewed economic concerns as a backdrop, dealers began to sell the dollar on published remarks by Duisenberg to a Spanish newspaper. He said the ECB was unlikely to buy dollars in order to temper the euro's gains, which hurts European exports and erodes growth in the 12-nation bloc.  \par \par \uc2 Though an ECB spokesman denied Duisenberg had commented on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders partly seized on the remarks as a signal to sell the dollar roundly.  \par \par \uc2 "After the payrolls report and Duisenberg's comments over the weekend, the market is doing a rethink of the dollar's moves. And with no follow-through (buying), the dollar's stance is for a further softening," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In afternoon U.S. trading, the euro  was up more than 1 percent on the\par day against the dollar, trading above $1.17. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par gains supported it against the yen, buying 129.80 yen          .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the Swiss franc , the dollar probed 1.32 francs, down 1.25 percent. The pound rose 0.70 percent to stand near $1.6750.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the dollar's surge on Friday, the greenback was in consolidation mode earlier in what was expected to be quiet trading on the Yom Kippur Jewish holiday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JOBS DATA GIVES LIMITED CHEER  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, the monthly read on the labor market showed nonfarm payroll jobs rose 57,000, outstripping economists' consensus forecasts for a loss of 30,000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts said that while the labor market report was encouraging with respect to the manufacturing and service sectors, it was not sufficient to reverse the market's bearish view on the dollar in the medium term.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Friday's (jobs) data was positive but again, it's only one piece," said John McCarthy, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Capital Markets in New York. He added that the perceived nonchalance about the dollar's weakness among global policymakers was also a factor.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The employment report certainly takes the edge off the dollar but it doesn't reverse the trend," he added. "We'll have to see string of better employment figures before you see people shifting from the current account concerns," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, German Finance Minister Hans Eichel, meanwhile, was quoted in French daily La Tribune as saying the euro zone must be careful that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements were not too rapid.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARKETS STILL ALERT ABOUT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was slightly weaker against the yen, trading around 110.808 yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were watchful of possible action from Bank of Japan to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar. In the past, the BoJ has been known to utilize thin markets to surprise traders by buying dollars with its considerable arsenal of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It may be to the BoJ's advantage to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a thin market," said Andrew Delano, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at IDEAGlobal in New York. "This wariness about the BoJ is keeping the dollar propped up against the yen," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031007dza6000tb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
