\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar tumbles as ECB's Duisenberg sees dlr decline.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier David  \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2003\par 22:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 6 (Reuters) - A broadly weaker dollar fell against the euro on Monday, after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said a decline in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "unavoidable," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to sell euros in order to halt its rise.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied after a report on the U.S. labor market showed the economy had added jobs unexpectedly in September. But because the economy has shed nearly 3 million jobs in the past two years, traders said the September gains was insufficient to reverse the labor market's weakening trend.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. markets were thin because of the Yom Kippur Jewish holiday. As a result, the dollar came under heavy pressure across the board after published remarks by Duisenberg added to traders' inclination to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In a Spanish newspaper, Duisenberg said the record U.S. current account deficit made a fall in the dollar "unavoidable." He added that the ECB was unlikely to buy dollars in order to temper the euro's gains even though a strong euro hurts European exports.  \par \par \uc2 Though an ECB spokesman denied Duisenberg had commented on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders interpreted the remarks as a new reason to sell the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "After the payrolls report and Duisenberg's comments over the weekend, the market is doing a rethink of the dollar's moves. And with no follow-through (buying), the dollar's stance is for a further softening," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In late U.S. trading, the euro  was up 1.20 percent on the day against\par the dollar, trading above $1.17. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gains supported\par it against the yen, buying nearly 130 yen          .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the Swiss franc , the dollar fell below 1.32 francs, to shed 1.38 percent on the day. The pound rose 0.40 percent to stand near $1.67.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JOBS DATA GIVES LIMITED CHEER  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, the monthly read on the labor market showed nonfarm payroll jobs rose 57,000, outstripping economists' consensus forecasts for a loss of 30,000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts said that while the labor market report was encouraging with respect to the manufacturing and service sectors, it was not sufficient to reverse the market's bearish view on the dollar in the medium term.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Friday's (jobs) data was positive but again, it's only one piece," said John McCarthy, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Capital Markets in New York. He added that the perceived nonchalance about the dollar's weakness among global policymakers was also a factor.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The employment report certainly takes the edge off the dollar but it doesn't reverse the trend," he added. "We'll have to see string of better employment figures before you see people shifting from the current account concerns," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, German Finance Minister Hans Eichel, meanwhile, was quoted in French daily La Tribune as saying the euro zone must be careful that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements were not too rapid.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARKETS STILL ALERT ABOUT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was little changed against the yen, trading around 110.80 yen .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were watchful of possible action from Bank of Japan to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar. In the past, the BoJ has been known to utilize thin markets to surprise traders by buying dollars with its considerable arsenal of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It may be to the BoJ's advantage to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a thin market," said Andrew Delano, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at IDEAGlobal in New York. "This wariness about the BoJ is keeping the dollar propped up against the yen," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031006dza60031q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
