\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Beggar Thy Euro Update  \b0\par\par\uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2003\par 19:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main message from G7 out of the Dubai meeting Sept. 20 was straightforward. Countries in Asia (China as well as Japan) needed to allow more of a role for markets in setting exchange rates so that the burden of current account adjustment (reducing the record U.S. current account deficit) can proceed and can be shared more evenly among key trading partners of the U.S. The Dubai communique, from a European perspective, should have translated into a rise in the yen versus the euro in the midst of a broad and orderly decline in the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Apart from about a week of euro/yen selling, this cross is back to where it was on Sept. 19 (around 129.60). Meanwhile euro/dollar is up nearly 3% (about what dollar/yen is down). From a European perspective, the Dubai communique is a failure. Japan continues to resist upward pressure on the yen and doing so by drawing lines in the sand (now at 110 down from 115 before G7) with unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This policy contributes to a higher euro/yen. It runs contrary to the very essence of the Dubai communique and belittles the significance of the Group. Why should markets respect G7 if Japan won't? Surely in periods of broad dollar declines like today, setting artificial floors in dollar/yen simply lead to a rise in euro/yen. The burden of adjustment rises for the Euro Zone while it is checked, or arguably declines for Japan. Hence the beggar thy euro charge.  \par \par \uc2 But it is not just Europe that is questioning \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. President George W. Bush on Friday said U.S. manufacturers deserve fair trade and fair \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies go with fair trade policies. President Bush was speaking to U.S. manufacturers and they have made no secret of their concerns about China's yuan peg as well as Japan's efforts at preventing a rise in the yen. Furthermore, Sen. Richard Shelby, R-Ala., a leading Republican in Congress who chairs the Senate Banking Committee, told the Japanese Finance Ministry's Zembei Mizoguchi earlier Monday that the yen is undervalued. Shelby will preside over a Senate Banking hearing on U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy Oct. 16, when Treasury Secretary John Snow will be questioned about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be the hot button topic China's yuan peg has become, but it is a close second in this jobless recovery and countdown to the 2004 U.S. presidential race.  \par \par \uc2 And then there is European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's prophetic remarks to the Spanish version of the Financial Times published Monday. Duisenberg said it is inevitable the dollar corrects (weakens) and he hoped and prayed it was gradual. Two things come to mind. Apart from Duisenberg's honesty (perhaps flushed out by his imminent retirement), the ECB President seemed to suggest that the massive U.S. current account deficit means the euro has loads more room to rise versus the dollar, and key to world financial market stability and ultimately growth is preventing it from becoming a dollar crisis. By resisting the Duisenberg inevitable outcome, Japan is contributing to a market distortion that elevates the risk of a dollar crisis...especially versus the euro.  \par \par \uc2 While we doubt the ECB and or Eurogroup is contemplating it, there is a compelling reason for the ECB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen as a seller. It would check some of the load the Euro Zone is shouldering while shifting it to Japan and it would be consistent with G7's general state of dysfunction.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.)  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031006dza6000c6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
