\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rallies as U.S.jobs data inspire.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 19:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar rallied against major counterparts on Friday after a surprisingly robust U.S. employment report for September.  \par \par \uc2 After a brief setback following a slightly weaker-than-expected U.S. service sector report, the dollar got a second wind, bruising the euro, which was down 1 percent against the greenback, and further punishing the Swiss franc, down 1.5 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Traders continued to keep one eye out for the possibility of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even with the dollar off the ropes against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now.  \par \par \uc2 By early afternoon in New York, the euro was down 1.06 percent to $1.1566. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3362 francs, up 1.53 percent. Against the yen, the dollar was trading at 110.89 yen, up 0.16 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The pound was down 0.53 percent to $1.6606.  \par \par \uc2 The Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing business index was 63.3 in September, compared with economists' forecasts for a reading of 62.5.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the ISM's service sector data, while coming in slightly lower than expected, were close enough to consensus forecasts not to undermine the dollar's overall bullish trend on Friday following strong payrolls data.  \par \par \uc2 "The ISM number did not impede the market's determination to buy the dollar in the wake of the payrolls report. It still points to healthy growth in the non-manufacturing sector," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But with all eyes on the state of the world's biggest economy's labor market, the main market-moving event of the session was still the U.S. employment report, which had started to lift the dollar early in the U.S. trading session.  \par \par \uc2 That appeared to offer some glimmers of improvement in the U.S. job market.  \par \par \uc2 "The report is a welcome surprise: good news for the U.S. economy," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The monthly jobs report showed nonfarm payrolls rose 57,000, outstripping economists' consensus forecast for a loss of 30,000 nonfarm payroll jobs, while the jobless rate remained at 6.1 percent, compared with economists' forecasts for an increase to 6.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "With this impressive turnaround in the latest jobs report ... things in the U.S. labor market ... are far better than we expected. Nonetheless, the market will have to wait until next month before they get confirmation" of sustained improvement, said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH  \par \par \uc2 The markets remained alert about possible yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though the greenback, which has been well-bid for most of the session, appeared to be enjoying a temporary reprieve against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders, however, were disappointed by the dollar's fairly lackluster bounce against the yen as the greenback failed to push decisively above 111 yen. "There has been a wall of offers for dollar/yen at the 110.75-110.80 area. And this is preventing the dollar from getting over that 111 yen barrier," said Philip Capone, senior trader at Fortis Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the dollar had hit three-year lows against the yen but recovered as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar hit three-month lows against the euro earlier this week as investors reassessed their views on the U.S. and global economies. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031003dza3002fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
