\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar up on US jobs, but gains limited.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against major rivals on Friday in a decisive reaction to a stronger-than-expected September U.S. employment report, but its gains were tempered after a report on the U.S. service sector came in close to consensus forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 The Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing business index was 63.3 in September, compared with economists' forecasts of a reading of 62.5.  \par \par \uc2 The main market-moving event of the session was the U.S. employment report, which pushed the dollar higher early in U.S. trading.  \par \par \uc2 "The report is a welcome surprise: good news for the U.S. economy," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The monthly jobs report showed nonfarm payrolls rose 57,000, outstripping economists' consensus forecasts of a loss of 30,000 nonfarm payroll jobs, while the jobless rate remained at 6.1 percent. Economists had forecast an increase to 6.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "With this impressive turnaround in the latest jobs report ... things in the U.S. labor market ... are far better than we expected. Nonetheless, the market will have to wait until next month before they get confirmation" of sustained improvement in the US jobs market, said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around 10:45 a.m. EDT(1445 GMT), the euro  was down 0.4 percent at\par $1.1640. Against the yen      , the dollar was virtually flat on the\par day at 110.66 yen. Against the Swiss franc      , the dollar was at\par 1.3278 francs, up 0.8 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound was down 0.1 percent at $1.6671.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the run-up to the jobs report, the dollar fell within half a yen of the three-year lows at which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively this week to weaken the yen, as investors fretted that the report might show more labor market stagnation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the stronger-than-expected jobs report sent the dollar clambering back against the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STILL ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WATCH  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lurking threat of possible yen-weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  still looms large in traders' minds, even though the dollar appeared to be off the ropes against the yen for the moment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, the dollar hit three-year lows against the yen but recovered as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  confirmed it had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The dollar hit three-month lows against the euro this week as investors reassessed their views on the U.S. and global economies.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the world's biggest economy's prospects at center stage, the U.S. labor market has risen to prominence in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders' minds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All the sensors within the economy are looking at jobs and the problems with job creation," said Peter Fontaine, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While questions still surround just how robust the U.S. economic recovery is, Japan appears to be exceeding economic growth expectations. That is putting strong upward pressure on the yen that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government is seeking to curb via \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in Friday's session, the dollar scraped session lows around 110.27 yen, below levels at which Japanese authorities were widely believed to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week, following confirmed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday near 110 yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That level is widely perceived by traders as the new "line in the sand" below which Japanese authorities are likely to try to check the dollar from falling. (Burton Frierson in London contributed to this report).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031003dza30025e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
