\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rallies on US jobs data upside surprise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 15:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against major rivals on Friday in a clear and swift reaction to a stronger-than-expected September U.S. employment report, which traders had hotly anticipated as the pivotal global data event of the week.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rebounded against the yen, pushed the euro down to the $1.16 area and rose 1 percent on the day against the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 With all eyes on the state of the world's biggest economy, and market participants braced for the possibility of a weak number, the report appeared to offer some glimmers of improvement in the U.S. employment market.  \par \par \uc2 "The report is a welcome surprise: good news for the U.S. economy," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The monthly jobs report showed nonfarm payrolls rose 57,000, outstripping economists' consensus forecasts for a loss of 30,000 nonfarm payroll jobs, while the jobless rate remained at 6.1 percent, compared with economists' forecasts for an increase to 6.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Signs of some improvement in the U.S. labor market "is one of the last pieces to come about in the recovery and is one of the pieces of evidence for a stronger dollar going forward over the next few months," Brickman added.  \par \par \uc2 Around 9.18 a.m. EDT Friday morning in New York, the euro was down 0.7 percent to $1.1614. Against the yen, the dollar was up 0.2 percent to 110.84 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3308 francs, up 1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The pound was down 0.3 percent to $1.6644.  \par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the jobs report, the dollar fell within half a yen of the three-year lows at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively this week to weaken the yen, as investors fretted that the report might show more labor market stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 But the more robust than expected jobs report sent the dollar clambering back against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar pushed up against the yen to 110.77 yen in initial reaction to the jobs report, released at 8:30 a.m. EDT (1230 GMT), from 110.44 yen immediately prior to its release.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had hit three-year lows against the yen on Tuesday but recovered as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar hit three-month lows against the euro this week as investors reassessed their views on the U.S. and global economies.  \par \par \uc2 With the world's biggest economy's prospects at center stage, the state of the U.S. labor market has risen to prominence in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' minds.  \par \par \uc2 "All the sensors within the economy are looking at jobs and the problems with job creation," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 While questions still surround just how robust the U.S. economic recovery is, Japan appears to be exceeding economic growth expectations. That is putting strong upward pressure on the yen that Japan's government is seeking to curb via yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in Friday's session, the dollar scraped session lows around 110.27 yen, below levels at which Japanese authorities were widely believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, following confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday near 110 yen.  \par \par \uc2 That level is widely perceived by traders as the new "line in the sand", below which Japanese authorities are likely to try to check the dollar from falling.  \par \par \uc2 Among other U.S. data, the Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing survey for September is due at 10 a.m. EDT (1400 GMT). (Burton Frierson in London contributed to this report).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031003dza3001xw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
