\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar near danger zone vs yen, jobs data ahead.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 13:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Friday within half a yen of the three-year lows that saw \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively this week to weaken the yen, as investors worried a U.S. report may show more labour market stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 The September U.S. employment report due at 1230 GMT is forecast to show a loss of 30,000 jobs versus August's loss of 93,000. Unemployment is seen up at 6.2 percent, from 6.1 percent, and likely to remain a key worry for the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit three-year lows against the yen on Tuesday but recovered as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar also hit three-month lows against the euro this week as investors reassessed their views on the U.S. and global economies.  \par \par \uc2 "The payrolls issue is the main problem that everyone sees - people are not hiring, they are firing - and you don't see a turnaround. As long as you don't see that, the dollar is set to look weak," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "All the sensors within the economy are looking at jobs and the problems with job creation."  \par \par \uc2 By 1130 GMT, the dollar fell as low as 110.27 yen within a quarter-yen of three-year lows.  \par \par \uc2 At $1.1700 per euro, the dollar was still close to three-month lows around $1.1770.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was below levels against the yen at which Japanese authorities were widely believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, following confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday near 110, seen as the new line in the sand for Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week but when they don't the dollar is sinking. We are again in the danger zone," said Fontaine.  \par \par \uc2 A chorus of Japanese officials, worried that a higher yen could derail the country's export-led economic recovery, have been warning on a daily basis that volatility in exchange rates was undesirable and Japan would act against it as needed.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. JOBS DATA  \par \par \uc2 The main focus on Friday was the monthly U.S. jobs data, given concerns a soft labour market could take a toll on consumer spending, the main driver of the U.S. economic recovery over the years.  \par \par \uc2 News this week that U.S. factory activity expanded for the third month in a row was dimmed somewhat by the fact that factory owners stepped up layoffs, according to the Institute for Supply Management's September report.  \par \par \uc2 "Overall the dollar is under threat. The recovery is not as strong as perhaps people had hoped. Almost every U.S. report disappointed this week," said Chris Gothard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Brown Brothers Harriman.  \par \par \uc2 "The payrolls report is a really important number. I think people will have positioned for downside risk."  \par \par \uc2 Also on Friday, the U.S. Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing survey for September is due at 1400 GMT, forecast to have fallen to 62.5 from 65.1 but still well above the 50 threshold that indicates expansion.  \par \par \uc2 A similar survey of euro zone's dominant service sector showed it shifted up a gear last month to its fastest pace since April 2002, as confidence soared and demand rose.  \par \par \uc2 The Reuters euro zone business activity index rose further above the 50 line that divides growth from shrinkage to 53.6 in September from 52.0 in August, the third month of expansion and above a 52.5 consensus forecast.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in Tokyo, Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said yen strength would not have a major impact in the next six to 12 months from a macroeconomic standpoint.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that upbeat economic data, such as Wednesday's "tankan" business survey, must be discounted by taking into consideration the recent surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031003dza3001ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
