\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar dips vs yen as Japan stays out of market.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 05:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The dollar edged down against the yen on Friday amid doubts about a U.S. economic recovery and the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities to sell their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The market keeps worrying about when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but once the authorities are away from the market, the yen starts to edge higher, and that's the case right now," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was mired within one percent of three-year lows around 110.10 yen and three-month lows around $1.1770 to the euro with traders cautious ahead of the closely watched U.S. employment report for September due out at 1230 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the spectre of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains.  \par \par \uc2 "As we near 110 yen, the market naturally gets anxious about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 At 0304 GMT, the dollar was trading at 110.72/75 yen down from the day's high of 111.00 yen and late U.S. level of around 110.75 yen - near levels at which Japanese authorities were widely believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  two days ago.  \par \par \uc2 A chorus of Japanese officials, worried that a higher yen could derail the country's export-led economic recovery, have been warning on a daily basis that volatility in exchange rates was undesirable and Japan would act against it as needed. "Japan must be concerned that large manufacturers in the latest tankan expected an average rate of 117.53 yen for the second half of the year, which is only a slight change from 117.79 yen in the June tankan," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at around $1.1692/96, barely changed from around $1.1695 late in New York.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. JOBS DATA  \par \par \uc2 Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Friday that yen strength would not have a major impact in the next six to 12 months from a macroeconomic standpoint.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that upbeat economic data, such as Wednesday's "tankan" business survey, must be discounted by taking into consideration the recent surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The main focus on Friday was the monthly U.S. jobs data, given concerns that a soft labour market would eventually take a toll on consumer spending, the main driver of the U.S. economic recovery over the years.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters survey of economists on non-farm payrolls has produced a median forecast for a loss of 30,000 jobs in September compared with a loss of 93,000 jobs in August. The unemployment rate is seen at 6.2 percent, up a notch from 6.1 percent. The Dow Jones industrial average rose 0.20 percent on Thursday despite weak economic data, including a 0.8 percent drop in U.S. factory orders in August, compared with a 2.0 percent rise a month earlier. But traders said the bearish sentiment on the dollar was widespread and even a positive surprise in the non-farm payrolls figures might only support the dollar for a very short while.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031003dza3000h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
