\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: More Or Fewer US Jobs?  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Markets On Hold Until 1230 GMT Friday \par  \par DJIA          9487.80    gain 18.60     up 0.2% \par NASDAQ        1836.22    gain 3.97      up 0.2% \par S&P 500       1020.24    gain 2.03      up 0.2% \par Dow Future    9457.00    loss 10.00     dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1336.50    loss 2.00      dn 0.2% \par S&P Future    1018.75    loss 0.75      dn 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.00% gain 0.04 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street ended a mostly indecisive session with modest gains Thursday as investors, still uneasy about stocks' high prices, hesitated to extend the previous session's huge rally. Treasurys lost a bit in price ahead of the all-important U.S. labor report Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Analysts said investors, despite their nervousness, are concerned about missing out on the next bull market and so are more inclined to buy stocks than sell.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems to be somewhat tentative," said Peter Cardillo, president and chief strategist of Global Partner Securities Inc., of Thursday's trading. "But nevertheless, I kind of think what we are seeing is people trying to get in the market who held out in the month of September.... Investors want to be long in the market."  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: U.S. Treasurys prices were modestly lower Thursday, with the market trimming earlier losses ahead of Friday's release of the September employment report.  \par \par \uc2 Economic data including initial jobless claims and August factory orders took a back seat to position adjustments ahead of payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 The 10-year note yield traded between 3.95% and 4.07%, before closing modestly lower for the session around 4.00%.  \par \par \uc2 A slew of comments on the economy and the outlook for job growth by various Federal Reserve officials also contributed to the ebb and flow of prices during the session, noted traders.  \par \par \uc2 However, the session was dominated by the proximity of Friday's key data report, which "potentially constitutes a turning point for the bond market," said Jay Mueller, economist at Strong Capital Management, Milwaukee.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Banks Lead Tokyo Shares Modestly Higher \par  \par USD-Yen             110.79       gain  0.07    up 0.05% \par AUD-USD             0.6850       gain  0.0008  up 0.1% \par Nikkei 225          10661.31     gain  67.78   up 0.6% \par Hang Seng           11580.92     gain  34.60   up 0.3% \par JGB Yield           1.3900% down 0.0350 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks held onto modest gains toward the close Friday, with bank issues fueling the rise following Wall Street's uptick.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors were reluctant to buy ahead of a U.S. jobs report for September.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Major banks UFJ, Sumitomo Mitsui Financial, Mizuho and Mitsubishi Tokyo Financial were behind the advance. The bank sector has benefited from signs of a recovery in the economy and a rallying stock market, which is helping to improve their balance sheets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, the dollar was holding around the 111-yen level, supported by this week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Japanese government.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy minister said Friday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves won't have a big negative effect on the economy over the next 6 to 12 months, but the impact of the yen's rise on exporters needs to be closely watched.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Over six months to a year, I don't see a very large effect from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Though there is an effect on exporters," Heizo Takenaka told reporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Grip For Possible US Jobs Jolt \par  \par Euro-USD            1.1694      gain  0.0002  up 0.02% \par Stlg-USD            1.6711      gain  0.0019  up 0.1% \par USD-Franc           1.3192      gain  0.0016  up 0.1% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares, government bonds and the euro are all expected to open little changed ahead of the 1230 GMT U.S. employment report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares may start the session slightly higher ahead of the U.S. labor figures. But many investors may elect to stay on the sidelines, leaving volumes light.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were mixed Thursday as gains in banking and technology shares offset continuing worries about the global economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philip Shaw, economist at Investec said, "Our underlying view is that euro-zone rates will have to come down by Christmas given the softness in underlying euro-zone inflation, the euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rebound, and lackluster economic growth."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ExxonMobil Corp. (XOM) is in negotiations to buy a stake worth as much as $25 billion in Russia's premier oil group, YukosSibneft, the Financial Times reports on its Web site.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Electric Co.'s NBC is expected to sign a definitive agreement as early as Friday to buy the Vivendi Universal SA film and television assets for $14 billion in equity, solidifying the pact the two companies reached a month ago and inching closer to ending months of negotiations.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are expected to open little changed or slightly lower, but essentially directionless until the U.S. employment figures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bonds fell Thursday, as the market finds it difficult to extend its rise beyond its month-long rally.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apart from technical factors, the selloff was largely sentiment-driven. "Everyone seemed to be in a selling mood," said Peter Schaffrik, analyst with Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And news from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate-setting meeting and press conference in Lisbon offered little support for bonds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB President Wim Duisenberg signaled that rates are likely to stay on hold for a long time, downplaying the recent rise in the euro and oil prices. He also sounded more confident on the outlook for economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro opens the session just under $1.1700, about where it left off at the New York close.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers pared back bets against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ahead of a keenly-awaited U.S. employment report Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They also digested comments from a series of Federal Reserve officials, which generally reflected optimism on the U.S. economic recovery, but also concern that the labor market remains weak.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed Governor Ben Bernanke said the output recovery remains on track for the second half of 2003 and continuing into 2004, but the weak employment scenario is "a major concern," while Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow said the job market is weak despite an upturn in household data.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And it is jobs data that are set to drive direction in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in the very near term. "The market's just taking a rest (in preparation) for what is the major data release," said T.J. Marta, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Citibank in New York, noting that "positioning is getting a bit oversold for the dollar."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citibank is forecasting a 50,000 drop in September non-farm business payrolls, while the consensus among economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for a 25,000 decline. If the data are in line with Citibank's forecast, "it probably won't be a great day for the dollar," Marta added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Friday, Oct 3: Services Purchasing; US Labor Data \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0245/0645 France   Second-Quarter Current Account \par 0300/0700 U.K.     September Purchasing Managers' Services \par 0350/0750 France   September Purchasing Managers' Services \par 0355/0755 Germany  September Purchasing Managers' Services \par 0430/0830 U.K.     September Official Reserves \par 0830/1230 U.S.     Sept Non-Farm Payrolls (seen -25K) \par 0830/1230 U.S.     Sept Jobless Rate (seen 6.2%; last 6.1%) \par 0940/1340 U.S.     September ECRI Inflation Gauge (last 117.6) \par 1000/1400 U.S.     September ISM Non-Manufacturing Index \par 1445/1845 U.S.     Federal Reserve Governor Olson speaks   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Philip Morris (R.PMO), Papastratos (I.PCG): E.U. antitrust decison \par Note: Philip Morris Holland B.V. a subsidiary of Philip Morris International will buy a 75% stake in the Greek tobacco firm Papastratos. Philip Morris is willing to pay a maximum of EUR18.15 per Papastratos share, but reserved the right to reduce the price by 1%, after due diligence. \par  \par KLM (KLM): September traffic data \par  \par Banco Guipuzcoano (E.GUI): To Pay 2003 EUR24 Dividend \par  \par Banco Sabadell (E.SBD): To Pay 2003 EUR0.24 Dividend \par  \par Vidrala (E.VID): Analyst Presenta