\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS-Wall Street drive sets pace for Asia.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Kaufman  \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Strong gains in U.S. stocks fueled Asian share market rises on Thursday, prompting more selling of the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars despite some early suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei index finished the morning up 1.18 percent at 10,483.18 led by banks and insurers. Nissan Motor Company jumped 2.75 percent, leading exporters higher. On the domestic side, convenience store Seven-Eleven Japan soared 4.9 percent.   \par \par \uc2  MSCI's dollar-based index of Asian share markets outside of Japan jumped 1.38 percent by 0220 GMT, lagging a 2.02 percent gain on Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose, with traders citing Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But after a sharp half-yen bounce, the dollar plodded back towards its recent lows.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit rose as high as 111.30 yen after falling earlier to within a half percent of Tuesday's three-year low of 110.10. The dollar bought 110.93 yen. Since the beginning of August, the dollar has lost 7.6 percent of its value against the yen.   \par \par \uc2  The euro bought 130.43 yen and 1.1760 dollars.   \par \par \uc2  "Although the authorities say they are not looking at a specific exchange rate level, I think they are worried about what would happen if they allowed the dollar to fall below 110," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager of the bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  department at Daiwa Securities SMBC.   \par \par \uc2  "If they pushed up the dollar aggressively, it could raise eyebrows of their overseas counterparts, so they seem to be just supporting the dollar around that level."   \par \par \uc2  The Nihon Keizai Shimbun financial daily reported that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was considering expanding the borrowing limit for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account - the war chest for its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - by up to 20 trillion yen ($180.7 billion) from 79 trillion yen currently.   \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones average and the Standard & Poor's 500 index jumped 2.09 percent and 2.23 percent respectively, their biggest one-day gains since June, boosted by a better-than-expected manufacturing report. The Nasdaq Composite Index rose 2.54 percent after losing more than 2 percent on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  WARY OF BOUNCES   \par \par \uc2  Asian traders such as Tu Jin-lung, vice president at Grand Cathay Securities in Taiwan, expressed caution about the early rises.   \par \par \uc2  "It is largely because of the rebound in U.S. stocks but we don't expect the market to rise sharply before we are sure U.S. stocks have stablised," said Tu.   \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's TAIEX was up 1.44 percent in relatively light volume.   \par \par \uc2  South Korea's stock market jumped 0.98 percent on the back of a 1.75 percent jump in index heavyweight Samsung Electronics, while Australian miners exposed to the U.S. economy led the benchmark S&P/ASX 200 0.89 percent higher to 3176.9, recouping Wednesday's losses.   \par \par \uc2  Singapore's Straits Times Index was 0.96 percent higher at 1,647.74, led by a 1.65 percent jump in Singapore Press Holdings, and a 1.03 percent gain in Contract manufacturer Venture Corp.   \par \par \uc2  NYMEX crude oil futures recovered early losses that were blamed on profit-taking to reclaim New York levels, with the key November futures contract at $29.43.   \par \par \uc2  Spot gold firmed to $385.25 after slipping in New York, with dealers expecting the precious metal to hold in a range of $383 to $385 an ounce.   \par \par \uc2  Reuters Messaging:   \par \par \uc2  rm:// chris.kaufman.reuters.com@reuters.net   \par \par \uc2  Email: chris.kaufman@reuters.com   \par \par \uc2  Telephone: +65 6870 3852)).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031002dza2000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
