\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Edges Higher vs. Euro and Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 23:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar edged slightly higher against most of its major counterparts Thursday, as dealers pared back bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a keenly awaited U.S. employment report Friday.  \par \par \uc2 After sharp moves in Asian and European trading earlier in the day - which included another apparent wave of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities - the dollar recovered in a relatively quiet New York session.  \par \par \uc2 That rebound was aided by a benign weekly U.S. jobless claims print, although weak factory-orders data kept a lid on any bullish sentiment. Dealers also digested comments from a series of Federal Reserve officials, which generally reflected optimism on the U.S. economic recovery, but also concern that the labor market remains weak.  \par \par \uc2 Fed Governor Ben Bernanke said the output recovery remains on track for the second half of 2003 and continuing into 2004, but the weak employment scenario is "a major concern," while Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow said the job market is weak despite an upturn in household data.  \par \par \uc2 And it is jobs data that are set to drive direction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the very near term. "The market's just taking a rest [in preparation] for what is the major data release," said T.J. Marta, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in New York, noting that "positioning is getting a bit oversold for the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Citibank is forecasting a 50,000 drop in September nonfarm business payrolls, while the consensus among economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for a 25,000 decline. If the data are in line with Citibank's forecast, "it probably won't be a great day for the dollar," Marta added.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday, however, was a better day, with only sterling advancing on the dollar. The upward revision this week to United Kingdom economic growth and merger and acquisition speculation are lending a strong bid tone to the pound, observers say.  \par \par \uc2 In late trading in New York Thursday, the euro was at $1.1692, down from $1.1720 late Wednesday in New York. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also at 129.53 yen, down from 129.65 yen. The dollar was at 110.79 yen, up from 110.64 yen late Wednesday in New York, and was also at 1.3174 francs, up from 1.3147 francs. Sterling was at $1.6714, up from $1.6665.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar got an early boost in Asian hours after the Bank of Japan, via agent banks and on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold yen for dollars to stem the yen's appreciation. Indeed, dealers in London and the U.S. said it looked like the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  also through another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, much in the same way it acted via the Federal Reserve Bank of New York Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In its latest round of dollar-buying, the Japanese government scooped up as much as $3.5 billion, according to dealers' estimates.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Mexico's peso closed sharply weaker against the dollar in volatile trading, falling 1.6% in the session as a poor outlook for the country's economy pushed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lows not seen in almost seven months.  \par \par \uc2 In Mexico City on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the peso closing at a midrate of 11.19 pesos per dollar, weaker from its open of 11.1340 pesos per dollar and from 11.01 peso at Wednesday's close.  \par \par \uc2 This was the weakest level for the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 5, when it ended at a record low of 11.2220 pesos per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A reduction in economic growth projections from Finance Minister Francisco Gil on Wednesday and a possible debt-rating downgrade of state-owned oil monopoly Pemex sparked dollar buying, pulling the peso as low as 11.20 pesos in intraday bids.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Santiago Perez in Mexico City contributed to this story.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031002dza200010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
