\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Euro Volatilities Ease Amid Rangebound Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 19:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options continued to decline Thursday in New York as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept to the middle of its range for the day.  \par \par \uc2 The euro reached a fresh 3 1/2-month high around $1.1770 amid continued dollar weakness overnight, but has since settled back around $1.17.  \par \par \uc2 "Options volatilities have been hammered" since the euro came off its highs," said an options dealer. "People were getting out of their short-term options strategies."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, volatilities implied by one-month dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options have also been on the decline in recent sessions, as Japanese monetary authorities have shown their resolve in defending the key Y110 level.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again in Tokyo trading, buying as much as $3.5 billion on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  via agent banks when the dollar dipped as low as Y110.54 and sending the dollar up almost a full yen.  \par \par \uc2 However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retested its lows in the afternoon session.  \par \par \uc2 Around 12:30 EDT (1630 GMT) Thursday, the dollar was at Y110.67, versus Y110.64 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1706, compared with $1.1720 Wednesday. It was also at Y129.54 from Y129.75.  \par \par \uc2 One-month at-the-money euro/dollar options implied volatility was at 11.30%/11.60%, from 11.40%/11.65% in Tokyo and 11.50%/11.90% in New York Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 11.00%/11.40%, little changed from 11.10%/11.45% earlier in Tokyo, but down from 11.70%/12.10% in New York Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month euro/yen option volatilities were at 10.65%/10.95%, down from 10.85%/11.25% earlier in Tokyo and Wednesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor yen calls at 2.9%/3.4%, unchanged from Wednesday. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied.  \par \par \uc2 Euro calls were favored by 0.7%/1.1% Thursday, versus 0.7%/1.0% the day before.  \par \par \uc2 Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1077 4127 44427  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031002dza2000r3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
