\par\par \b \uc2 Euro Ousts Yen As Dollar Bears' Choicest Bet  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Adriana Arai  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 16:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Japan's renewed commitment to capping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has offered dollar bears a timely, short-term trade. Some are already abandoning bets on a stronger yen in favor of the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Until this week, most investors saw the yen as a safer vehicle than the euro to profit from general weakness in the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Many took a call from the Group of Seven richest nations for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes as a signal that Japan would stop trying to cap the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But they were wrong. Sentiment has changed after Japanese authorities stepped in with heavy yen selling on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "A lot has changed over the past 48 hours," said Michael Burton, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales to institutional investors at Goldman Sachs in London. "Many dollar/yen shorts were flushed out by the BoJ moves."  \par \par \uc2 Traders think the BoJ has bought $8 billion worth of dollars for yen since early Tuesday to keep the dollar above Y110. And more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks likely.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears strong yen appreciation will add to deflationary pressures and could even abort its embryonic, export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Traders and fund managers have had to rethink short-term tactics, given that charts now suggest further euro gains are likely in the short term after the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  put on a massive three U.S. cents this week alone.  \par \par \uc2 The yen is still expected to rise in the medium term. But technicians say the latest surge clears the way for the euro to break the all-time high of $1.1929 achieved in late-May.  \par \par \uc2 Other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Swiss franc and the British pound are also benefiting against the dollarfrom the switch away from yen longs.  \par \par \uc2 Even so, systematic hedge funds - big players in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that rely on chart-driven computer models to trade - still have bigger bets on a rise in the yen than in the euro, said Burton. But that may no longer be true for other types of leverage funds, he added.  \par \par \uc2 As investors piled into the euro again, the 12-nation single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a fresh 15-week high of $1.1766 during Asian trading Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital analyst Phil Roberts sees further gains soon if the euro stays above $1.1720. Around 1400 GMT the euro was off its highs, changing hands at $1.1677, down from $1.1729 late Wednesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Rates Favor Euro \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors have switched to long-euro from yen because they were losing money on the trade, said Dominique Corombelle, global markets products coordinator for Fortis Bank in Brussels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Either the market moves quickly and challenges the Bank of Japan, or you have to change strategy," he said. "Not everyone can afford to sit on (dollar/yen) shorts for too long."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More fundamentally, though, analysts are saying Friday's U.S. jobs data for September, if weak, will likely drive the euro higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global interest rate spreads, moreover, favor the euro trade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because U.S. rates are higher than in Japan, running a short dollar position against the yen - effectively borrowing the dollar to buy yen - results in a loss unless the dollar falls. A three-month position would cost investors around 1.1% on an annual basis.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But since euro rates are higher than U.S., shorting the dollar against the euro - that is borrowing the dollar to buy euro - can yield a 1% annual return - even if the pair stays steady.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the only risk here would be a European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut in interest rates if the euro surges too fast.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unlike the BoJ, though, the ECB isn't expected to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or ease monetary policy to weaken the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Target $1.20 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Determined to keep inflation under control, European policymakers have long desired to see the euro rise against the dollar. Not once did they threaten to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the euro jumped 14% earlier this year in just five months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors grew even more comfortable with their euro positions after a Wednesday media report that quoted unnamed ECB officials as saying they would only start worrying about the euro's strength after it goes above $1.20. Now traders say this is the near-term level they're aiming at.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest signs from Japan, in turn, have been less encouraging for investors betting on a yen surge. Japanese authorities hinted earlier Thursday they're considering an increase in borrowing limits to fund further dollar/yen buying by the end of the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Assuming traders' estimates for this week's BoJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are correct, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has spent a record Y14.4 trillion to prop up the dollar this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strategy this week has so far worked. The massive onslaught has stopped the yen from rising, even in the wake of foreign buying into Japanese stock market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With BoJ commitment at record levels, investors are well advised to buy European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207 842 9483 adriana.arai@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031002dza2000m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
