\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Ex-BOJ Nakagawa: Yen Rise Won't Cripple Japan  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Harui  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 11:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen's 6.5% surge against the dollar in the past month won't cripple Japan's economy, a former senior official at the Bank of Japan said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though there is an (adverse) effect, a stronger yen won't leave the Japanese economy floundering," Yukitsugu Nakagawa, a former executive director at the BOJ, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise, in theory, damages Japan's economic recovery by making its exports more expensive and reducing Japanese companies' foreign earnings. It also boosts deflationary pressure by making imports cheaper.  \par \par \uc2 But Japanese companies such as Toyota Motor Corp. (TM or 7203) have weathered sharp yen appreciations in the past and are competitive enough to bear another yen rise, Nakagawa said.  \par \par \uc2 The yen has risen rapidly against the dollar in recent weeks and hit a fresh 34-month high of Y110.07 Tuesday. Around 0934 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y110.84.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rise has spooked monetary authorities. The BOJ, on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has sold Y13.48 trillion, mostly for dollars, in the first nine months of this year in an effort to prevent an unbridled yen rise from snuffing out Japan's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities are widely believed to have sold yen Thursday for the third straight day, lifting the dollar nearly a yen after it fell as low as Y110.54.  \par \par \uc2 Nakagawa, now deputy chairman of the Institute for International Policy Studies, said the Finance Ministry is expected to continue its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend a suspected 'line in the sand' at Y110.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no more room for fiscal or monetary (stimulus), so there's a strong feeling (in the government) that a weaker yen is needed to spur the economy a bit," he said.  \par \par \uc2 As a result, the finance ministry is likely to brush off U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's call to keep aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a minimum, Nakagawa added.  \par \par \uc2 At a Sept. 20 meeting of the Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers in Dubai, the U.S. and other G7 nations called for flexibility in exchange rates - a signal that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders interpreted as implying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should scale back its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      BOJ May Tighten Monetary Policy In 2H Of 2004 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ is likely to maintain its super-loose 'quantitative easing' policy through the first half of next year amid lingering concerns about falling prices in Japan, Nakagawa said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may tighten monetary policy in the second half of next year as on-year changes in consumer prices could rise above zero and stabilize in positive territory if Japan's economic recovery continues next year, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "(Japan's economy) will likely grow 3% this year. If it grows 3% next year, prices will inevitably rise," Nakagawa said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since March 2001, the Bank of Japan has used the nationwide core CPI as its main deflation indicator, and has said it will keep its ultra-easy policy until the on-year change in that index stabilizes at or above zero.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deflation has long been Japan's key economic problem, exacerbating the banking system's bad debt problem and increasing the debt burden on restructuring firms. Sagging prices have also sapped the ability of the BOJ's super-loose policy to spark bank lending and economic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In August, Japan's core consumer price index fell 0.1% from a year earlier, extending the string of declines to a 47th month and ensuring the BOJ will keep its super-loose monetary policy on hold for now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakagawa said any monetary tightening move by the BOJ would be done with great care, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would thoroughly assess the factors behind any rise in consumer prices and may have to take into account other issues such as Japan's ballooning fiscal deficit before any policy change, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Bennett Richardson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031002dza2000e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
