\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD:China, Japan Still Big Muscle In Tsy Market  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Agnes T. Crane  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 962 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 23:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Don't be fooled by the "U.S." in the U.S. Treasury market.  \par \par \uc2 With most domestic investors focused on inflation and the labor market in the U.S. for the past few months, a recent statement from the Group of Seven industrialized nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy reminded everyone that there is another important factor behind historic low interest rates: buying by Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 To be more specific, the insatiable appetite of the Bank of Japan and the People's Bank of China for U.S. government securities has been instrumental in keeping Treasury yields at their historic lows.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of that support weakening was enough to cause investors to dump government securities last week, with some even wondering out loud whether the banks would start selling Treasurys. That fear evaporated once it became clear Japan and China aren't planning to pare back their appetite for Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Central to all of this is \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via its agent the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is the largest practitioner of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the industrialized world. Motivated by its wish to stop a strong yen from derailing a fragile economic recovery, Japan typically buys dollars and sells yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and parks those dollars in the Treasurys market. China, which keeps the yuan in an extremely narrow band against the dollar, has also become a major force in the U.S. bond market.  \par \par \uc2 By calling for more flexible exchange rates, the G7, intentionally or not, left markets with the distinct impression that they wanted a weaker dollar. In order to achieve this, the theory was, they specifically wanted Asian central banks to stop managing the value of their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The knock-on impact of this would be less demand for Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Almost two weeks after the G7 meeting however, the notion of that happening is fading. For one, market participants think Japan, which has already purchased well over $100 billion this year in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , still has money to put to work in the U.S. bond market.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Cheah, a portfolio manager who manages $1 billion in Treasury and mortgage assets at SunAmerica Asset Management in Jersey City reckons the Bank of Japan has only invested a fraction of the roughly $38 billion purchased in September, with the rest parked in cash temporarily. "Eventually this will find its way into the Treasury market," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Sleeping Giant Stirs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's monetary authorities did refrain from seeking to influence the yen's exchange rate in the week immediately following the G7 meeting, leaving many participants in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond markets with the impression that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had taken the communique to heart.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Then on Tuesday, it entered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets twice, once through third party banks and the other through the New York Federal Reserve Bank, making mince of the notion that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would allow its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to strengthen indefinitely.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China and its newfound love for Treasury notes, purportedly those with maturities of more than two years or longer, has received less attention, even though some believe the G7 communique was directed at the Middle Kingdom and its insistence on pegging the yuan to the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The combination of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg and an expanding trade surplus has transformed China into a massive buyer of Treasurys, a safe-haven instrument for a conservative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that needs to invest its burgeoning dollar reserves. Some estimates put China's Treasury holdings at over $100 billion, about one-quarter of Japan's.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's trade surplus with the U.S. reached $65.3 billion in the first seven months of the 2003, up 24.4% from last year and 71% larger than Japan's surplus of $38.1 billion. China, like Japan, is believed to pump the majority of its dollar holdings into Treasurys.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If China maintains its peg, which it insists it will do, the yuan will weaken along with the dollar against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , making its exports even more competitive and its surplus even larger. Any attempts by outsiders, such as the G7, to influence the nation's monetary policy, is likely to be met with resistance, say analysts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think they are going to try to rebuff any U.S. or G7 attempts to get them to re-value any time soon," said Scott Anderson, senior economist at Wells Fargo & Co, in Minneapolis. "All countries' policy tends to flow toward their direct economic interest."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Not All Things Stay The Same \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While few expect China to budge any time soon towards revaluing the yuan, Japan could slow or even stop its purchases of Treasurys if and when the economic recovery in the U.S. truly takes hold.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the dollar is falling, they buy a lot of bonds," said David Abramson, European strategist at BCA, a research group based in Montreal. "But, if the dollar stabilizes or moves higher...I think they would slow down their purchases or stop them."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If a true economic recovery takes hold, in which companies renew hiring and inflation begins to creep higher, instead of weakening further, the dollar could get a lift from foreigners moving back into the U.S. equity markets, says Abramson.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This would alleviate pressure on Japan to buy dollars, indirectly knocking out a key support for the bond market at the precise time when reliable buyers are already few and far between.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes Crane covers credit markets for Dow Jones Newswires)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020031002dza2000it\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
