\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  seen buying time with yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 1 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's renewed effort to halt the relentless advance of the yen, this time at 110 to the dollar, looks like a losing battle, coming within weeks of a breakdown of the previous defence line at 115.  \par \par \uc2 Yet analysts, at least those on the optimistic end of the spectrum, say it's not necessarily a disaster if the authorities lose the battle so long as they win the war by buying some time for corporate Japan to learn how to live with a higher yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The important thing is not the exchange rate levels themselves but the speed of the change," said Takehiro Sato, executive director at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \uc2 "If the yen's rise is gradual, Japanese companies can cope with it by rationalisation and restructuring, which they have done many times before."  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose to about 101 to the dollar in late 1999 and went as high as 80 in mid-1995.  \par \par \uc2 Taisuke Tanaka, chief macro strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston, agreed that buying time was important, but the crucial thing was what steps were taken during the moratorium.  \par \par \uc2 "If Japan just had your garden-variety economic problems, maybe it could let the yen rise and run its course. But in the current deflationary environment, a rapid yen rise would kill off all the positive development and dash hopes of recovery" he said.  \par \par \uc2 "You can't continue large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  forever," he added, saying that further monetary easing was crucial.  \par \par \uc2 DEFENCE LINE  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose to a three-year high around 110.10 to the dollar on Tuesday, having risen seven percent in just two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Behind the upward pressure on the yen were signs of a recovery in the Japanese economy and a 30-percent rally in the Nikkei stock price average over the past six months.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's current account surplus, totalling 8.9 trillion yen in the first seven months of the year, has also kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyant.  \par \par \uc2 Prior to the latest yen rise, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had for months prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from breaking above the 115 level and choking off the economy's export-driven recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that it had conducted 4.46 trillion yen of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, bringing the year-to-date total to a record 13.5 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 A September 20 statement by the Group of Seven industrial countries calling for flexibility in exchange rates had fuelled speculation that Japan now had its hands tied behind its back.  \par \par \uc2 But Tokyo ended a two-week hiatus from market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  loudly through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, a move seen by many traders as a show of Washington's tacit approval of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Still, the yen's retreat was modest and the unit was already back within sight of its three-year peaks on Wednesday afternoon, trading around 111.30 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 COMPLACENCY  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" business survey showed the country's major manufacturers were assuming a dollar/yen rate of 117.99 yen for their business plans in the year to March.  \par \par \uc2 Azusa Kato, an economist at BNP Paribas, said this suggested the companies were caught off guard by the recent yen rise, putting a question mark over an otherwise upbeat tankan report.  \par \par \uc2 "Having said that, an even bigger rise in the yen in 1999 did not affect big manufacturers' DI (diffusion index in tankan reports)," she added.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the MOF should lay off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only served to lure exporters into a false sense of security, evident in the tankan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate projection.  \par \par \uc2 But exporters would no doubt want to see a weaker yen.  \par \par \uc2 Nissan Motor Co Ltd (7201.T), for instance, says every one-yen drop in the dollar reduces its operating profit by 10 billion yen. Sony Corp (6758.T) says a one-yen move is worth 30 billion yen in sales and five billion yen in operating profit.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no question the yen's strength would affect the companies' bottom lines in the short term," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Over the longer term, however, a higher yen was not necessarily all negative, he said. A stronger yen made imported material cheaper, reducing costs, and could stimulate capital spending and consumption through increased purchasing power.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031001dza10014p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
