\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar stays sickly, BOJ seen  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  in vain.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoshiko Mori  \par \uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 08:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 1 (Reuters) - The dollar remained sickly in Asian trading on Wednesday, showing little sign of emerging from near three-year lows even when the Bank of Japan (BOJ) appeared to step in to shore it up, traders said. The BOJ, which acts as an agent for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appeared to have bought dollars at around 111.10 yen, they said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar responded by rising briefly by a quarter of a yen but failed to hang on to much of the gains.  \par \par \uc2 A senior MOF official declined to comment on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment for the greenback stayed bearish on the back of demand for yen not just from speculators but also by commercial accounts and investors.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little reaction to the Bank of Japan's closely watched "tankan" business sentiment survey released early in the day as its upbeat figures had already been factored in by the market, where the dollar fell to 110.07 yen on Tuesday, near a three-year low. Senior Japanese ruling party politician Hidenao Nakagawa, a close confidant of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, said he thinks the United States is basically sticking to a strong dollar policy. But the market was little impressed by his comment.  \par \par \uc2 Bearishness on the dollar has been underscored since Japan revealed on Tuesday evening that it bought nearly 4.5 trillion yen ($40.41 billion) in September. The sum was a record for a single month, but had little effect on the market.  \par \par \uc2 "How much time Japan will be able to buy before the dollar collapses is something that crossed many traders' minds when they saw the (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) amount and the shakey state of the dollar," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities Co.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 0615 GMT, the dollar  stood at around 111.25 yen       , down from\par late Tuesday's New York levels of about 111.50. It had fallen on\par Tuesday to 110.07 yen before spiking up to around 112 yen on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was also firmer against the dollar, which was broadly softer after weak readings in the Chicago-area purchasing managers' survey and consumer confidence numbers.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was quoted around $1.1675  and 129.80 yen\par         , although it was still below three-month highs above $1.1735\par set on Tuesday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEAD SCRATCHING AT MOF  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials, worried that the yen's latest surge could jeopardise the nation's export-driven recovery and undermine Tokyo share prices, kept up their rhetoric.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Japan was determined to stem irregular movements in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will take decisive action in an appropriate manner to stem irregular movements," he told a parliamentary committee.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi said Tuesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was aimed at correcting an overshooting in the yen exchange rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Stability is important and overshooting is inappropriate. We \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from that standpoint," the vice finance minister for international affairs told reporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Appropriate or not, the dollar remained bearish despite the fact that Japan's colossal dollar purchases match the size of its annual current account surplus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far this year, Tokyo has bought some 13.5 trillion yen worth of dollars, compared to Japan's projected current account surplus of approximately 14 trillion yen for the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders say officials at the MOF must be scratching their heads in dismay because they cannot remove downward pressure on the dollar despite their attempts to absorb dollar selling and yen buying demand arising from Japan's external imbalances.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The MOF's efforts have been partly offset by strong demand for yen and for Japanese assets by global investors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign investors have bought hefty 6.8 trillion yen worth of Japanese stocks so far this year, according to the MOF.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "U.S. and European pension funds have lately been the big buyers of the yen. They must be keen on Japanese equities and optimistic about Japan's economy," said a U.S. investment banker.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, the tankan report showed some improvements.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The survey's key diffusion index of sentiment for large manufacturers improved to plus 1 from minus 5 three months earlier, rising above the neutral zero mark for the first time in nearly three years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The index measures business sentiment by deducting the percentage of companies reporting unfavourable conditions from those that say they are favourable.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031001dza1000re\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
